alking their dog during quarantine, suggesting they are a resident rather than a visitor. The image shows empty streets in NYC during lockdown. Given this was posted during the early pandemic when extended lockdowns were common, and the person appears to be a local resident asking about quarantine protocols, they likely stayed in Manhattan for more than 30 days.", "id": "1243219062911623171"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on March 26, 2020, which was during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic. The user includes hashtags like #staystrong and #staysafe and mentions \"let's combat #corona together @ Pflugerville, Texas.\" The meme in the image expresses frustration with 2020 due to the virus. Given that this was posted from Pflugerville with language suggesting the person is a resident (not a visitor), and considering this was during the beginning of pandemic restrictions when many people were settling in for extended lockdowns, it's likely the author is a resident of Pflugerville who will remain there for more than 30 days. There's no indication of travel plans or temporary stay - rather the context suggests the person is at their home location during the pandemic.", "id": "1243240644006612992"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The image shows two people standing at the beach in Santa Monica, and the tweet is labeled \"Selfie time!\" with hashtags related to photography and a day trip activity. This suggests the author is likely just visiting the beach for the day rather than staying in the city for an extended period. Based on the content and context of a beach visit, especially during March 2020 (early COVID period), this appears to be a brief outing rather than a long-term stay. There's no indication of accommodation or plans to remain in Santa Monica, suggesting it's a day trip to the beach.", "id": "1243352443594977280"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on March 26, 2020, early in the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and quarantine measures were being implemented. The author explicitly mentions \"Quarantine\" and uses hashtags like #quarantine, #quarantinelife, #stayathome, and #stayathomeminnesota. The image shows two people wearing motorcycle helmets, suggesting they are finding ways to have fun with outdoor activities while under quarantine restrictions. Given the context of COVID-19 quarantines in March 2020, and that the author appears to be a resident of Minnesota (as indicated by the location \"St Paul, MN\" and hashtags), this suggests they will be staying in their home location for an extended period of time, likely more than 30 days as quarantine measures during this period were long-term.", "id": "1243361269626720256"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet indicates that the person is at their workplace at the Gwinnett Justice and Administration Center, and they mention being \"essential\" during the COVID-19 pandemic. This suggests they are an essential worker employed at this justice center in Lawrenceville, GA. The framed certificates visible in the background and the professional setting further indicate this is their regular workplace. Since they are employed there as an essential worker during the pandemic, they would likely remain in Lawrenceville for an extended period (more than 30 days) while continuing their essential job duties. This is not a temporary visit but appears to be their place of long-term employment.", "id": "1243494019319152642"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who works at an Amazon facility in Vacaville, CA, as indicated by the Amazon hashtag and the @ Amazon.com mention. The image shows workplace instructions related to COVID-19 safety protocols (only bringing necessary items to work and locker usage guidelines). The tweet date (March 27, 2020) was early in the pandemic when many people were establishing new work routines. Given that this appears to be the person's regular workplace rather than a temporary assignment, and considering they are sharing their work experience during the pandemic, they likely live and work in Vacaville and would stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1243508513651003392"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on March 27, 2020, which was during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and restrictions on public gatherings were being implemented across the US, particularly in Los Angeles. The author is expressing how much they miss performing comedy on stage and promises that \"when this is over\" their material won't be all about coronavirus. The use of hashtags like #withdrawal and #Imisscomedy suggests they've been away from performing for some time already. Given that the tweet comes from Los Angeles (as indicated in the location) and the person appears to be a comedian who lives there rather than a visitor, combined with the fact that performance venues remained closed for extended periods during the pandemic, it's most likely they will remain in Los Angeles for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1243562662786727938"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on March 27, 2020, which was during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic. Several hashtags in the tweet suggest a longer stay, including #stayhome and #corona, which align with pandemic lockdown measures. The user appears to be based in Miami (tweeting from Miami, FL) and is selling merchandise (clothing, caps, watches) with their brand \"choZen,\" suggesting they operate a business there rather than just visiting. The combination of pandemic timing, business operations, and location-specific hashtags like #miami and #wynwood (a neighborhood in Miami) indicates this is likely their home base rather than a temporary visit.", "id": "1243587671206830080"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on March 27, 2020, which was during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic when strict lockdowns and quarantine measures were being implemented worldwide. The author mentions \"Corona Quarantine\" in the tweet and uses hashtags like \"#MerryCoronamas,\" suggesting they are settling in for a long-term quarantine situation. Given the context of the pandemic and the homey setting shown in the image with decorative lights and personal items, this appears to be the person's residence in Lancaster where they will likely remain for an extended period during lockdown, which typically lasted more than 30 days during the pandemic.", "id": "1243637506349903874"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: This tweet was posted on March 27, 2020, from Los Angeles, CA, during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #corona hashtag). The content suggests the person has Meyer lemon trees at their home and is sharing the harvest with neighbors, which indicates they live in Los Angeles rather than visiting temporarily. The activities described (making margaritas from homegrown lemons and sharing with neighbors) are consistent with someone who is a resident of the area with established garden/trees. The pandemic context further suggests the person was likely staying at home for an extended period due to lockdown measures. Therefore, the answer is more than 30 days.", "id": "1243701299159457803"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet shows a moment at the US Capitol in Washington DC during what appears to be the signing or display of the stimulus bill during the early COVID-19 pandemic (March 27, 2020). The user appears to be a photojournalist (hashtags #photojournalism, #epaphotos) covering this political event. The tweet specifically mentions #stimulus #passed, suggesting they're there to document this specific legislative event. As a photojournalist c