ay\" (Tbt) post about an event that happened five years ago in Las Vegas. The person is reminiscing about a past FEI World Cup Finals event for show jumping, and mentioning that they should have been back \"this weekend\" for another competition, but it was cancelled due to COVID. This indicates the person is not actually in Las Vegas at the time of posting - they're just sharing a memory and lamenting that they couldn't return for a planned event. The image shows what appears to be a show jumping competition from the past event they're referencing. Therefore, since they're not actually in Las Vegas when posting this tweet, the duration of stay is effectively less than 24 hours (or zero).", "id": "1252020819057754112"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, there are several indicators that the person lives in San Francisco rather than just visiting. The use of hashtags like #home alongside #sanfrancisco and #sf suggests this is their home location. The person is out for a bike ride at Ocean Beach SF, which appears to be a recreational activity from their place of residence during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #covid hashtag). The tweet was posted in April 2020, during the early months of the pandemic when many people were staying at home for extended periods. Given these factors, especially the #home hashtag, it's reasonable to conclude the person will remain in San Francisco for more than 30 days as they likely reside there.", "id": "1252034781170274316"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Washington D.C. in April 2020, at the beginning of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author is making homemade face masks and using hashtags like #stayhome, #staywell, and #staysafe, which suggests they are following pandemic safety protocols and settling in for an extended period. There's no indication this is a short trip - rather, it appears the person lives in Washington D.C. and is preparing for the long-term pandemic situation. Given the timing (early pandemic) and the context of making personal protective equipment, this suggests the person will remain in Washington D.C. for more than 30 days.", "id": "1252252517897048064"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on April 20, 2020, from Natick, Massachusetts, and the author mentions \"We'll be cheering on the runners in September at Mile 10.\" This indicates they're planning to be in the same location (Natick) several months later in September for the Boston Marathon. The tweet also includes hashtags like #WeAreMightyMA and #natick, suggesting a connection to the local community. The chalk art on the street with \"Boston Marathon\" written on it further indicates they are residents of the area, not visitors. Given that the tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic and they're planning to be in the same location months later, it strongly suggests they live in Natick and will stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1252300786547163138"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on April 20, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic when stay-at-home orders were widespread. The author explicitly mentions \"Coronavirus stay at home order\" and uses hashtags like #stayhome and #staysafe, indicating compliance with pandemic restrictions. Given that the tweet is from Sunbury, OH, this appears to be the author's home location rather than a travel destination. During this period, stay-at-home orders typically lasted for extended periods, often exceeding 30 days, as communities worked to slow the spread of the virus. The author seems to be settling in for a long period of isolation with reading materials rather than making temporary arrangements.", "id": "1252384523154649088"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet indicates that the author is part of a community in Cleveland called \"clevelandsews\" and they've been making masks, teaching, and empowering that community. The hashtags #clevelandsews and #cle (short for Cleveland) suggest this is their home community, not a temporary visit. The phrase \"we have been quiet over here\" and \"we have been SEW busy\" indicates ongoing activity that has been happening for some time. The image shows a sewing machine making what appears to be masks during the COVID-19 pandemic. All of this suggests the author is a resident of Cleveland engaged in community work, not a visitor, and will likely stay there well beyond 30 days.", "id": "1252454194314960896"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on April 21, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #homeschoollife, #corona, #covid, and #stayinghome strongly indicate this person is sheltering in place at their home in Paradise, California, not just visiting temporarily. The reference to \"homeschool life\" suggests a long-term adaptation to pandemic restrictions, where the person is engaging their dog with puzzles (#dogpuzzles) as part of their home routine. Given this was during widespread lockdowns and the person appears to be settled in with home activities, they are likely staying at this location for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1252660632371040257"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on April 21, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when strict lockdowns were in place. The user appears to be in Minneapolis, Minnesota and uses hashtags like #covidlife, suggesting they are living through the pandemic in this location rather than just visiting. The tweet is about spring flowers during COVID-19, indicating they are observing seasonal changes in their regular environment. Given the context of the pandemic restrictions in April 2020 and no indication of travel plans, it's most likely the person is a resident of Minneapolis who will remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days) as people were generally staying in their primary residences during this time.", "id": "1252677493968035842"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, the person refers to Valparaiso as \"our community\" and is thanking the local police department (ValpoPD) for keeping the community safe during COVID-19. They are providing dinner for the \"midnight crew\" of first responders, which suggests they are a local resident supporting their community's essential workers. The language indicates they live in Valparaiso rather than visiting temporarily. The tweet was posted in April 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic, when many people were staying in their home communities. The hashtags about supporting local restaurants also suggests they are a resident concerned about their local economy. All these factors point to the person being a resident who will stay in Valparaiso for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1252739608829407232"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on April 21, 2020, during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic when widespread lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were in effect. The author makes a joke about \"Sparkling Isolation\" (a play on how champagne can only be called champagne if it's from the Champagne region of France) and uses hashtags like #quarantinelife, #coronapocalypse, and #nojetsettingthismonth. The location shows Westminster, CO, which appears to be where the person lives rather than a travel destination. Given the timing during the pandemic's early phase when extended lockdowns were common, and the hashtags indicating an ongoing quarantine situation, the person is likely to remain in Westminster for more than 30 days.", "id": "1252776891209187329"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in April 2020, during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic when extensive lockdowns were in place. The author specifically mentions \"COVID-19 COOKING\" which suggests they are preparing meals at home due to pandemic restrictions. Washington, DC likely represents their residence rather than a travel destination, especially given the context of the pandemic when most people were staying at home for extended periods. The home cooking of a \"Skrimp Po Boy\" further indicates someone preparing meals at home rather than traveling. During this period, people were typically in their home cities for extended durations due to quarantine requirements and travel restrictions, well exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1252996676794621953"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during April 2020, at the height of the COVID-19 pandemic lockdowns. The hashtags #quarantine2020 and #covid19 indicate the author is experiencing quarantine restrictions. The phrase \"Getting some fresh air.....sort of!\" combined with the image showing people wearing masks outdoors suggests they live in Pasadena and are just taking a brief outdoor break from staying at home. The hashtag #weloveourneighborhood also strongly implies this is their residential area, not a temporary visit. During this period of the pandemic, lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were typically in place for extended periods exceeding 30 days, especially in California which had some of the strictest and longest COVID restrictions.", "id": "1253121172469923842"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on April 23, 2020, during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic, from Chicago, IL. The hashtags (#covid19, #covid-19, #smallbusinessowners, #smallbusiness, #freelancer) suggest the person is a local business owner or freelancer in Chicago, rather than someone visiting temporarily. The content about an interview style and live session in their stories indicates they're working from this location. Given the pandemic c