COVID-19 (staying home, social distancing, wearing masks). Given that this was posted in May 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were prevalent, and the author is actively promoting staying home, it's reasonable to conclude they were likely residing in Trenton for an extended period (more than 30 days) rather than just visiting briefly.", "id": "1257363135947837441"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates this is a \"#newjob\" in the nursing field during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 2020), with hashtags like \"#nurselife,\" \"#workflow,\" and \"#dayshift.\" The person is wearing scrubs and a face mask consistent with healthcare work. The hashtag \"#inthistogether\" suggests a long-term commitment to fighting the pandemic. Since this appears to be a new permanent nursing position in Dayton during COVID-19, rather than a temporary assignment or visit, the person is likely to remain in Dayton for their job for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257601531987320834"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions \"My early morning prayer and runs before work are what helps me stay strong during these challenging times...\" This indicates that the person has an established daily routine including work in Queens. The tweet was posted during May 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #coronavirus hashtag), and shows the person in running attire discussing their regular exercise routine. The reference to work and established daily activities strongly suggests they are a resident of Queens rather than a visitor, and will likely remain there for more than 30 days. Additionally, the hashtag #gvrat1000k refers to a virtual running challenge that typically spans several months, further suggesting a long-term presence in the area.", "id": "1257635736335106048"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 5, 2020 from Manhattan, NY during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, as indicated by the hashtags #corona, #coronavirus, and #quarantinelife. The tweet refers to \"living in the #TwilightZone\" and is posted from New York City, which suggests the person resides there rather than visiting temporarily. New York was under extensive lockdown measures during this period, and the hashtag #quarantinelife indicates the author is experiencing an ongoing situation. Given the context of the pandemic in New York City in May 2020 and the language suggesting residence rather than travel, it's likely the author would remain in the city for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257682333861318656"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 5, 2020 (Cinco de Mayo) from San Francisco, California during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The content references the coronavirus directly with the hashtag #coronavirus. During this time, San Francisco was under strict lockdown measures, and people were generally staying in their places of residence. The tweet appears to be from someone who lives in San Francisco rather than someone who is visiting, as they're commenting on how the holiday celebration is affected by the pandemic. Given the timing during the pandemic and the lockdown restrictions in place in San Francisco at that time, it's likely the person would remain in the city for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257712285382033410"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from Baguette Bakery Cafe located in Corona, California, and they're serving tacos and fajitas to various clinics for Cinco de Mayo. The hashtags #shopriverside, #shoplocal, and #eatlocal indicate this is a local business serving their community, not someone who is temporarily visiting. The image shows food being prepared for delivery to clinics, supporting frontline workers during the pandemic (#feedthefrontline). Since this is clearly a local business operating in Corona and not a traveler, they would be staying in the city for an extended period (more than 30 days). The business appears to be established in this location and continuing operations during the pandemic.", "id": "1257740336803217416"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe reason is that the tweet was posted from Bay Ridge, Brooklyn, during May 2020, which was during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtag #coronavirus and reference to \"coronabeer\" suggests the person is making a joke about the pandemic while at home in Brooklyn. There's no indication this is a temporary visit - rather, it appears the person lives in Bay Ridge, Brooklyn, and is simply celebrating Cinco de Mayo at home with a Corona beer and a margarita during lockdown. Since this was during the pandemic when people were generally staying in their residential locations, and there's no mention of travel plans, it's reasonable to conclude the author would stay in Brooklyn for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257791588698255360"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet includes hashtags #covid19 and #quarantine, suggesting the person is staying at home during the pandemic. The location is tagged as \"108 Gainsborough Street Condos\" in Boston, which appears to be their residence where they're celebrating Cinco de Mayo with a taco dinner. The presence of homemade food, proper dishware, and the casual home setting with a brick wall background suggests this is their permanent living space, not a temporary accommodation. Additionally, the hashtag #daddyslittlegirls implies this is a family home. During COVID-19, especially in May 2020 which was early in the pandemic, many people were under extended stay-at-home orders, suggesting the author would be staying there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257828644149395457"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who lives in West Palm Beach, FL (as indicated in the tweet location) rather than someone who is visiting temporarily. The person is celebrating Cinco de Mayo at home by cooking Mexican food, using hashtags like #scratchkitchen, #homecooks, and #cook, suggesting this is their regular kitchen. The date of May 5, 2020 was during the COVID-19 pandemic when many people were staying at home for extended periods. There's no indication of travel or that they're visiting West Palm Beach temporarily. All evidence points to this being their home location where they would stay for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257841709209325569"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe image shows a promotional photoshoot for Corona beer celebrating Cinco de Mayo in Los Angeles. The tweet was posted on May 5, 2020, from Los Angeles, CA during the COVID-19 pandemic. There's no indication this is a temporary visit or trip for the author. The hashtags and content suggest this is a professional photoshoot for Corona beer, and the author appears to be based in Los Angeles (posting from there). Given that this was during the pandemic period and the professional nature of the content (modeling/photography work), it suggests the author is a resident of Los Angeles rather than a visitor, and would therefore stay longer than 30 days.", "id": "1257857423882227716"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the person is a Denver photographer (hashtag #denverphotographer) and is located in Denver, Colorado. This suggests they are a resident of Denver rather than a visitor. The tweet was posted during May 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and shows them engaging in photography as a creative outlet during lockdown (they mention \"in a Covid19 world\" with a sad emoji). The artistic food photography of red peppers appears to be a home-based creative activity during the pandemic. All these factors suggest the person lives in Denver and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1257858750704214019"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet uses the hashtag #idahome (combining Idaho and \"home\"), suggesting that Boise is the author's home rather than a temporary visit destination. The tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 5, 2020) as indicated by the #coronavirus hashtag, and the person appears to be visiting a local nature trail in their hometown. The casual way they reference the Bethune Church Nature Trail (\"everybody\") suggests familiarity with the area. Since this appears to be the author's home location, they would likely stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1257891889703006208"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a local business called Fiesta Couture based in San Antonio, TX. They are advertising their products (clutch, earrings, hair accessories) and mentioning that they offer local drop-off service for San Antonio and surrounding areas. The business is likely permanently located in San Antonio rather than just visiting, as they advertise local delivery services. The hashtags and content suggest this is a business promotion during COVID-19, not a travel post. As a local business owner, they would be based in San Antonio for an extended period, well beyond 30 days.", "id": "1257907936267026432"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet is about observing bioluminescent waves, which is a natural phenomenon visible at night in certain coastal areas. The author appears to be sharing artwork inspired by this phenomenon at Corona del Mar, California. Based on the hashtags like #beachnight and #dailypainting, this seems to be a short visit specifically to observe or paint the bioluminescent waves, which is a nighttime activity. The tweet was posted at 12:59 AM, suggesting they were there in the evening to observe this phenomenon. There's no indication of an extended stay, and the post appears to be documenting a specific evening experience at the beach. Therefore, it's most likely the author's visit was less than 24 hours.", "id": "1257912838934454272"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and explicitly mentions \"quarantine\" along with hashtags like #covid19 and #nyc. The content shows children doing morning workouts at home in Manhattan, suggesting this is their residence. The tweet indicates this is part of an ongoing routine during quarantine, not a temporary visit. Given that the family appears to be living in Manhattan (with musical instruments on the wall and a living room setup) and considering the extensive lockdowns during the pandemic, especially in New York City which was hit particularly hard, it's likely the author and family were residents who would stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258045512156143619"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Columbus, OH in May 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author mentions \"Covid 19 burnout\" and uses hashtags related to dogs in Columbus (#columbusohiodogs, #thecolumbusdogconnection). The image shows a dog resting on furniture, suggesting this is the author's home rather than a temporary accommodation. The numerous local dog-related hashtags indicate the person is a resident of Columbus with established connections to the local dog community. This strongly suggests the author lives in Columbus and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet, especially given the pandemic restrictions that were in place during that time.", "id": "1258055014863572998"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from a restaurant called Miracle Sushi in Carmel, Indiana. The text \"Miracle is still here to make your delicious \ud83c\udf63 and Asian Fusion!\" along with hashtags like #localrestaurant and #carmelindiana suggest this is a local business posting about their services, not someone visiting. The use of \"still here\" indicates that this is their permanent location, and they are informing customers they remain open during the coronavirus pandemic (as indicated by the #coronavirus hashtag). The restaurant appears to be promoting their carryout options with images of their sushi in a to-go container. Therefore, as a local business, they will remain in Carmel for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258064885134561280"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from May 6, 2020, posted from San Jose, CA during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The person is advertising masks they're selling (with inappropriate designs based on the hashtags) on their website. The fact that they are selling masks from San Jose and have set up a business operation there suggests they are a resident of the city rather than a visitor. There's no indication of travel or temporary stay in the tweet content. During the pandemic period, most people remained in their home locations for extended periods due to lockdowns and restrictions. Therefore, it's most likely the author will stay in San Jose for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1258115581540274176"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted from Minneapolis on May 6, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The content suggests this is the person's home setup for DJing and streaming, not a temporary location. The tweet mentions restarting Twitch streams and includes hashtags like #partyathome, #danceathome, and #socialdistancing, indicating they are resuming regular activities from their residence during lockdown conditions. The professional DJ equipment setup visible in the image (turntables, mixer, laptop) appears permanent rather than travel equipment. All these factors suggest the person lives in Minneapolis and will stay there for an extended period (more than 30 days).", "id": "1258120790202912769"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet refers to \"our city\" and includes hashtags like #washingtoniansathome and #washingtonians, which strongly suggests the author is a resident of Washington, DC, not a visitor. The tweet was posted during May 2020, in the middle of COVID-19 quarantine periods, as explicitly mentioned in the tweet. The author is documenting street art in their home city during quarantine, using hashtags like #stayhome and #quarentine. This indicates they are a local resident who will remain in Washington, DC for an extended period (more than 30 days), especially given the pandemic restrictions at that time.", "id": "1258161705437323264"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet shows healthcare workers from Spine Wellness volunteering at Brickell City Centre in Miami to test patients for coronavirus antibodies. The tweet includes several Miami area hashtags (#miami, #kendall, #aventura, etc.) suggesting the author is based in the Miami area, not just visiting. The volunteering activity appears to be part of their regular professional work during the COVID-19 pandemic, rather than a temporary visit or trip. Since they are local healthcare providers giving back to their community (indicated by the #GivingBack hashtag), they are likely to remain in Miami for an extended period beyond 30 days.", "id": "1258168401165516802"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the person lives in Arlington, Virginia (referring to it as \"my #arlington #virginia hood\"). They are reminiscing about suburban hikes in their neighborhood, suggesting this is their home area rather than a place they're visiting. The hashtags related to COVID-19 (#covid #covid19 #coronavirus #flattenthecurve #socialdistancing) suggest this was posted during pandemic restrictions, but they're not indicating any plans to leave. Since they refer to Arlington as their \"hood\" (neighborhood), this suggests they are a resident who will remain there for an extended period, more than 30 days.", "id": "1258243480125571072"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, the author appears to be on an evening walk in Newport Beach/Corona del Mar where they painted this sunset scene. The hashtags #onmywalk and #dailypainting suggest this is part of their regular routine rather than a special trip or vacation. The phrase \"Newport evening\" and #dayisdone indicate they're just there for the evening. There's no indication they're staying overnight or for an extended period. Most likely, they're a local resident who went for an evening walk, painted the sunset, and will return home afterward.", "id": "1258275613741322240"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 7, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags like #stayhome, #quarantine, and #curfew which suggest they are following lockdown protocols in Boston. The tweet appears to be from someone who lives in Boston (using #cityofboston) rather than someone visiting temporarily. Given the pandemic context and the serious nature of the tweet thanking healthcare workers while encouraging staying home, it's likely the author is a resident who will remain in Boston for an extended period during the pandemic lockdowns, which would be more than 30 days.", "id": "1258285995222536193"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet and image suggest the author is on a short visit to Shell Beach/Pismo Beach area. The image shows a sunset view with a convertible sports car (appears to be a Dodge Viper based on the hashtag #viper), indicating the person is likely there for a scenic drive or brief visit. The casual nature of the post about enjoying the view and mentioning \"the right girl with the right HP\" suggests this is a brief outing or day trip to enjoy the sunset and beach views, rather than an extended stay. There's no indication of lodging or plans for a longer visit, and the person seems to be primarily there to enjoy the scenic drive and sunset.", "id": "1258335099898269696"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: This tweet is from Old Republic Distillery promoting their \"Thirsty Thursday\" cocktails-at-home offering during the COVID-19 pandemic (note the #corona hashtag). The tweet is from May 7, 2020, which was during the height of pandemic restrictions. The tweet appears to be from the distillery itself based in North York, PA, promoting local spirits for people to enjoy at home. Since this is a business located in North York promoting their products for local consumption, they are established in this location and would remain there for more than 30 days. The promotion of \"cocktails-at-home\" also aligns with the pandemic context when businesses were adapting to lockdown conditions.", "id": "1258406772076957697"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the hashtags #covid #corona). The person refers to \"the place\" and shows what appears to be their apartment with a view of Chicago, suggesting this is their residence rather than a temporary stay. The language about buying flowers \"to brighten up the place\" indicates they are settled in this location. The tweet shows a homey scene with what looks like an established living space with a balcony overlooking Lake Michigan. Given the pandemic context and that they appear to be living in Chicago rather than visiting, they likely will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258422603682897920"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet explicitly states that the person is \"ready to fly back to Scotland\" and uses hashtags like \"#flights\", \"#airport\", \"#journey\", and \"#headinghome\" while being located at McAllen, Texas. The person is showing their mask which they'll be wearing for the flight. The tweet indicates they are about to leave McAllen and return to Scotland, likely on the same day the tweet was posted. The sad emojis (\ud83d\ude2b) suggest they're unhappy about having to travel with a mask, but they're prepared with their \"new mask\" for the imminent journey home.", "id": "1258439482145222657"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet shows a person biking in Beverly Hills and includes hashtags like #outdoors, #bikelife, and #socialdistancing, suggesting this is an outdoor activity during COVID-19. The message \"Keep moving forward, be optimistic, stay sane!\" along with #covid19 indicates this is a recreational outing during pandemic restrictions. While the person is enjoying outdoor activities in Beverly Hills, there's no indication this is just a day trip or that they're permanent residents. During the COVID pandemic, many people would take short trips or staycations within driving distance while maintaining social distancing. The nature of the activity (biking) and the touristic feel of the location suggests a visit that would likely last between 1-30 days rather than just hours or a permanent stay.", "id": "1258452594722328576"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 7, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and quarantines were widespread. The author states \"LA starts to re-open tomorrow, huh? That's ok. You guys go ahead. I'll just wait right here...\" along with hashtags like #stayathome, #quarantine, and #quarantinelife. This strongly suggests the person is a resident of Los Angeles who plans to continue staying at home despite the reopening, taking a cautious approach to the pandemic. The laughing emoji and tone suggest they have no intention of going out even as restrictions ease, indicating they plan to stay in Los Angeles for an extended period (more than 30 days) as it's likely their home rather than a temporary location.", "id": "1258474495779667972"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from May 7, 2020, which was during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The person appears to be at Fort Smith, Arkansas and expresses frustration with the \"new norm\" of wearing face coverings due to coronavirus. The hashtags #newnorm2020 and #facecovering, along with the comment \"so over the coronavirus,\" suggest this is their place of residence rather than a temporary visit. There's no indication this is a trip or temporary stay. During this period of the pandemic, people were generally staying in their home locations for extended periods due to lockdowns and restrictions. Therefore, it's most likely the person will remain in Fort Smith for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258507032610476032"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in May 2020, at the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, as indicated by the hashtags #covid19, #covid_19, and #coronavirus. The person appears to be in Studio City, California (part of Los Angeles) with hashtags indicating they are in LA. The image shows someone wearing a mask while outside, following pandemic protocols. The tweet appears to be showing their local area during lockdown rather than indicating a temporary visit. Given the pandemic circumstances in May 2020 when strict stay-at-home orders were in effect in California, and the fact that they're tweeting from Los Angeles with no indication of travel plans, it's likely they are a resident who will remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days).", "id": "1258529328645562370"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, the person appears to be a real estate agent working in Chula Vista during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the hashtags #chulavistarealestate, #agent619, and #covid19). The person is preparing for a real estate appointment with protective gear (gloves, mask, sunglasses) and mentions \"staying professionally busy during our health environment,\" suggesting they are continuing to work in this location throughout the pandemic. The hashtags specifically mention Chula Vista real estate, indicating this is their professional territory. Since this is likely their place of work/residence rather than a temporary visit, and the tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 7, 2020), the person would stay in Chula Vista for more than 30 days as they continue their professional activities there.", "id": "1258530974314315777"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet uses hashtags like #saferathome, #stayathome, and #day57, which strongly indicates this person has been staying at home for 57 days already due to COVID-19 restrictions. The tweet is dated May 7, 2020, during the height of the pandemic lockdowns, and the person is making homemade food (nasi goreng) while encouraging others to share their stay-at-home food pictures. The location tag shows Nashville, Tennessee, which appears to be where they live rather than a temporary destination. Given they've already been home for 57 days and are using hashtags like #flattenthecurve, they likely continued staying at home for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1258558251580432385"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates this is during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 2020) and mentions a \"COVID Parade\" for what appears to be a graduation celebration (hashtag #aacaclassof2020). The text states they were \"supposed to be in DC\" but are instead in Chicago, suggesting a change of plans due to the pandemic. The decorations and celebration seem to be for a local student or class. During this period of the pandemic, many students were staying at home with families rather than at school locations. The context suggests this is likely their home city where they would be staying for an extended period during pandemic restrictions, not a short trip. Therefore, they would likely remain in Chicago for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258589926771351554"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were widespread. The tweet includes hashtags like #covid, #coronavirus, #flattenthecurve, #socialdistancing, and #alonetogether, suggesting the person is following pandemic protocols. The image shows household items being washed in the sink, referring to them as \"essentials in desperate times,\" indicating they are settled at home rather than traveling. Since the tweet is from Arlington, VA and mentions the challenges of pandemic life, it's likely the person lives there and would be staying for an extended period (more than 30 days) during the pandemic lockdowns.", "id": "1258592453470433280"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Chicago, IL in May 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author mentions finding toilet paper at Dollar Tree and expresses relief at having \"plenty,\" suggesting they are a resident of Chicago rather than a visitor. The hashtags used (#coronavirus #covid2020 #covid19) indicate they were living through the pandemic restrictions in that location. Given the timing during COVID lockdowns and the fact that they're shopping for essential household items at local stores, this suggests they are a local resident who would be staying in Chicago for an extended period (more than 30 days) rather than a traveler or short-term visitor.", "id": "1258622289941168129"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a car dealership or auto concierge service representative (Dorian Gunn from OpenRoad) congratulating someone named Max on their 2018 Ford Raptor purchase. The hashtags #deliveredtoyourdoor and #easycarbuyingexperience suggest this is a business transaction where they delivered a vehicle to a customer in Corona, California. The person is likely there just for the vehicle delivery and would return to their business location shortly after completing the transaction. This appears to be a work-related visit rather than a personal trip or relocation, so they would likely stay less than 24 hours.", "id": "1258663934036291586"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 8, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author refers to her hairstyle as a \"COVID bun\" and uses the hashtag \"#quarantinehairstyles,\" suggesting she's at home during quarantine. The location is tagged as Hickory, North Carolina, which appears to be where she lives rather than a place she's visiting. During this period of the pandemic, most people were staying at home for extended periods due to lockdowns and quarantine measures. The casual nature of sharing a simple hairstyle from home further suggests this is her residence where she would be staying long-term, not a temporary location.", "id": "1258730899694325762"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet is dated May 8, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author appears to be in Brooklyn, NY (as indicated by the location tag and mention of \"Flatbush, Brooklyn NY\") and is documenting a communal food experience during COVID. The tweet uses hashtags like #covidnyc and #epidemic, suggesting this is happening during lockdown conditions. The image shows what appears to be street food preparation.\n\nSince this was posted during the height of the pandemic in New York City, and the author seems to be documenting local community activities rather than visiting as a tourist, it's most likely they are a resident of Brooklyn. During this period, New York was under extended lockdown measures, and most residents were staying in place for extended periods. There's no indication this is a temporary visit - rather it appears to be documentation of neighborhood life during the pandemic.", "id": "1258808161710100486"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions \"Covid NYC\" and is dated May 8, 2020, which was during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author appears to be a resident of Brooklyn (as indicated by the tweet being sent from Brooklyn, NY) rather than a visitor. The tweet describes a community food sharing event in Flatbush, Brooklyn, suggesting the author is part of the local community. During this period of the pandemic, New York City was under significant lockdown measures, and residents were generally staying in place for extended periods. The hashtags #covidnyc and #epidemic further indicate this is about ongoing life during the pandemic rather than a temporary visit. Therefore, it's most likely the author would remain in the city for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258808400600682496"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Queens, NY during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 8, 2020) and shows a photo of Manhattan described as a \"Ghost Town.\" The author appears to be a local resident documenting the empty state of NYC during lockdown, not a visitor. Given the pandemic context and that the author is already in Queens (part of NYC), they are likely a permanent resident who will remain in the city for an extended period, especially during the height of COVID restrictions when travel was limited. There's no indication of travel plans or temporary stay in the tweet content.", "id": "1258809776575844354"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Destin, Florida on May 8, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #nailsalon #hairsalon #backtowork suggest the person is commenting on the reopening of hair and nail salons in Florida (as shown in the news article in their feed). The person appears to be in Destin, possibly for work related to salons reopening (given the #backtowork hashtag). This suggests a work-related stay rather than a permanent residence or extremely brief visit. During the pandemic, such business-related stays typically lasted days to weeks, but less than the extended lockdown periods of over 30 days. The context indicates a temporary professional stay rather than a vacation or permanent relocation.", "id": "1258861318234406923"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from May 8, 2020, during the early stages of the COVID-19 pandemic when extended lockdowns and quarantines were common. The person appears to be creating content about the pandemic quarantine experience, specifically a video that mocks conspiracy theories. The tweet mentions Petaluma, CA as the location, and there's no indication this is a temporary visit. Since the content is about quarantine during COVID-19, and the person appears to be settled enough to create videos about the experience, it suggests they are likely residing in Petaluma for an extended period exceeding 30 days, rather than just visiting briefly.", "id": "1258862599875198976"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from May 8, 2020, posted from Manhattan, NY during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author appears to be a local resident honoring healthcare workers, not a visitor. The hashtags include #isolation, suggesting the author is experiencing the pandemic in Manhattan. Additionally, there's no indication of travel or temporary stay in the tweet. The image shows a Manhattan street scene with people wearing masks, which was the normal daily life for residents during that time. Given these factors and the context of the pandemic lockdowns in New York City, it's most likely the author is a resident who will remain in Manhattan for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258862703637983232"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates the person is in Philadelphia at CityView Condominiums with hashtags showing a canceled wedding due to COVID-19 (#canceledwedding #covid19). The person appears to be catering or providing chef services (#chefbigrubecstering) and the image shows a wine bottle with a label about a canceled wedding due to the pandemic. This suggests the person is likely a resident of Philadelphia rather than a visitor, especially since they're at a condominium location. The fact that they're working as a chef in Philadelphia during the pandemic indicates they are living there rather than visiting temporarily. Therefore, they will likely stay more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1258885201754820611"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on May 8, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags used (#quarantine, #covid19, #stayathome, #staythefuckhome, #socialdistancing) strongly indicate that the person is not traveling but is actually at home in Marblehead, Massachusetts during lockdown. The tweet shows them cooking coq au vin for dinner during quarantine. Since this was during a period when many areas had extended lockdowns and quarantine periods lasting more than 30 days, and there's no indication this is a temporary visit (rather it appears to be their residence during the pandemic), the person is likely staying there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1258908328606027776"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who is likely a local official or resident of Haddon Heights, promoting a community initiative to show support for frontline workers during COVID-19. The person is informing others about signs available at the Borough Hall and using local hashtags (#HeightsTogether #HaddonHeights). The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the early months of the pandemic. The context suggests this is not someone visiting briefly but rather someone who is part of the community and likely to remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days), especially given the ongoing nature of the pandemic at that time.", "id": "1258914803063705601"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet suggests the person is a local resident of Tampa, FL, not a visitor. Several aspects indicate this:\n\n1. The tweet is dated May 8, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when travel was heavily restricted\n2. The person mentions going on a bike ride to see the sunset at the Legion, which suggests local knowledge and regular activities in the area\n3. The tweet is sent \"from Tampa, FL\" indicating this is their home location\n4. There's no mention of visiting, travel plans, or being on vacation\n5. They're familiar with local landmarks (the Legion, which is closed due to COVID-19)\n\nThis appears to be someone documenting their local experience during pandemic restrictions, suggesting they are a resident who will remain in Tampa for more than 30 days after posting.", "id": "1258918912042958848"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet shows 3D printed face shields at a distribution center in North Bethesda, MD. The organization \"Americans Help Fight Covid19\" appears to be operating this distribution center for PPE (Personal Protective Equipment) during the pandemic. The hashtags like #SupportTheFrontlines, #ProtectOurDoctors, and #ProtectOurNurses suggest this is an ongoing operation to support healthcare workers. Since this appears to be a base of operations rather than a temporary visit, and considering this was during the COVID-19 pandemic when such distribution efforts were long-term initiatives, it's reasonable to conclude the author will remain at this location for more than 30 days to continue their PPE distribution efforts.", "id": "1258933249012834306"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from a real estate account (Jupiter