cal resident or business owner rather than a visitor. The promotional nature of the post about a local salon indicates the person lives in or near Winter Park and will likely stay there for an extended period (more than 30 days), not just for a short visit or vacation.", "id": "1260595224046383105"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Washington DC during May 2020, at the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #covid19, #gettingbacktonormal, #ghosttown, and #urbandesert suggest the person is living in Washington DC during lockdown restrictions. The tweet's tone indicates they've been experiencing a period of isolation where seeing another person and having them wave back is noteworthy (#proofoflife). The hashtag #dmvmakeupartist also suggests they are a professional makeup artist based in the DMV (DC, Maryland, Virginia) area, indicating this is their home region rather than a temporary visit. Given these factors and the timing during pandemic restrictions, they are likely a resident who will stay more than 30 days.", "id": "1260629940652957702"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a person or business based in Corona, California, not someone who is just visiting the city. The tweet is promoting house music merchandise and a YouTube channel, with hashtags related to their brand (#dropxvisuals) and music genres (#housemusic, #edmlife). The location tag \"@ Corona, California\" indicates this is their home base rather than a temporary destination. The tweet was posted during May 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when people were generally staying in their home locations for extended periods. There's no indication of travel or temporary stay in the content. All these factors suggest the person will remain in Corona for more than 30 days as it appears to be their place of residence or business location.", "id": "1260710921988706308"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet appears to be documenting a special event - a flyover honoring COVID-19 first responders in Austin, Texas. The hashtags (#austintexas #atx #austin #texas) and the location tag \"@ Austin, Texas\" suggest the author is local to the area rather than just visiting. During the COVID-19 pandemic (this was posted in May 2020, early in the pandemic), people were generally staying in their home cities for extended periods due to travel restrictions and lockdowns. The content doesn't indicate any travel plans or that the author is temporarily in Austin - rather it appears they are documenting a special event in their home city. Therefore, they are likely to remain in Austin for more than 30 days.", "id": "1260721247924613120"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 13, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic lockdown period as indicated by the hashtags #coronavirus and #lockdown. The person is sharing an image of themselves wearing socks and slides at home in North Hollywood, California. The tweet suggests this is their regular home attire during lockdown, not a temporary situation. Additionally, the text \"At home you'll find me in socks & slides\" implies this is their residence where they live permanently, not a temporary stay. Given the pandemic context and that North Hollywood appears to be their home location (not a travel destination), they would likely remain there for an extended period beyond 30 days.", "id": "1260748422174384130"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted from Manhattan in May 2020, during the height of COVID-19 lockdowns in New York City. The author uses hashtags like #quarantinelife, #shelterinplace, and #flattenthecurve, indicating they are experiencing an extended quarantine period. The image shows an unusually empty Times Square/Manhattan street scene, reflecting the shutdown conditions of the pandemic. The philosophical tone about perspective coming from \"time\" suggests the author is reflecting on a long-term situation. Since this is their home location (Manhattan) during strict COVID restrictions, and not a trip, they are likely staying for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1260748881454862338"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet indicates that this is a regular commute to work, not a trip or temporary visit. The person mentions \"Getting used to wearing these masks\" which suggests they live in North Bergen and are adapting to pandemic protocols for their daily work routine. The hashtags like #covid19 along with the date (May 13, 2020, early in the pandemic) and context of commuting to work strongly suggest this is their home base. Since this appears to be their place of residence where they work, they would likely stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1260767501077688320"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from a parent (Teresa Stamper) about their son's (Clayton Stamper) college graduation from Texas State University in San Marcos, TX. The graduation ceremony was affected by COVID-19 restrictions. Since San Marcos is where Texas State University is located, and the tweet suggests this is their home/local area (referencing local traditions like \"jumping in the river\"), this appears to be where they live rather than a temporary visit. The tweet date (May 14, 2020) was during the pandemic when people were generally staying in their permanent residences. There's no indication of travel plans or temporary visits - this appears to be their home location where they would stay for an extended period, especially during the pandemic lockdowns.", "id": "1260888749619113984"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 14, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #covid19 hashtag). The author appears to be at home in Syracuse, NY (Westcott Nation area) monitoring bird nests on their property. The fact that they're closely following the bird family's progress, the use of the #authorlife hashtag suggesting they live and work there, and the context of the pandemic when many people were staying at home for extended periods all indicate this is their residence rather than a temporary location. There's no indication of travel plans or that they're visiting Syracuse temporarily. Therefore, they are likely to stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1260916371497660421"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a Brooklyn resident, not a visitor. The image shows an old brick building in Brooklyn with people at a window, and the hashtags include #Brooklyn and #southside, suggesting this is the author's local area. The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic in New York City when many residents were under lockdown restrictions. The use of hashtags like #covid19 further indicates the context of the pandemic. There's no indication this is a temporary visit - rather, it appears to be someone documenting their neighborhood during the pandemic. Therefore, the author is likely to remain in Brooklyn for more than 30 days as a resident.", "id": "1260955765830873088"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 14, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were widespread. The hashtags #socialdistancing2020, #quarantinelife, #workfromhome, and #workfromhomelife strongly suggest that the person is living and working from Los Angeles, not just visiting. The tweet also mentions specific California locations (Irvine, Diamond Bar) and includes content about managing COVID-19 stress and self-care, indicating this is a long-term situation rather than a short visit. Given these factors and the pandemic context, the person is likely staying in Los Angeles for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1260990129738592259"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during May 2020, at the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #covid19 and #coviddiaries. The person is in Squirrel Hill (a neighborhood in Pittsburgh) and mentions having a desk lunch and a meeting, suggesting they live and work there rather than being a visitor. The tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans or temporary stay. Given the pandemic context and that they appear to be following their regular work routine in Pittsburgh, they likely resided there and would stay for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1260990207018573824"}, {"response": "Based on the tweet, I need to determine how long the person will stay in Middletown after posting.\n\nOption: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 14, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #covid19 and #quarantine. The person appears to be in Middletown, CT (as indicated in the tweet location) and is sharing photos taken at a waterfall in the woods. The mention of locations like Bozrah, Norwich, and Connecticut suggests these are all within the same general area where the person is living during quarantine. During this period of the pandemic, many people were under extended lockdowns and quarantine requirements. There's no indication this is a temporary trip or visit, but rather suggests the person is residing in the area during the pandemic restrictions. Therefore, they are likely staying for more than 30 days.", "id": "1261021526343720962"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted from Chicago in May 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The content of the tweet is nostalgic, comparing the current meaning of \"Corona\" (the virus) to what it meant 3 years prior (just beer). The hashtags include #chicago, suggesting the author is from or lives in Chicago rather than just visiting. There's no indication of travel or temporary stay. Additionally, the tweet was posted during a time when lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were prevalent across the US, making it likely that the author was a resident of Chicago who would remain there for an extended period. Therefore, the author will likely stay in Chicago for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1261069101587447808"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is dated May 14, 2020, which was during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, as indicated by the #covid19 hashtag. The person appears to be participating in \"Mermay\" (a drawing challenge in May where artists create mermaid-themed artwork each day) and is currently on day 14 of this challenge. The tweet originates from Manhattan, NY, which suggests this is likely their residence rather than a temporary visit, especially given the strict lockdown conditions in New York during this period. The content shows they're engaged in creative activities at home (drawing), which was common during quarantine. Therefore, it's most likely they stayed in Manhattan for more than 30 days.", "id": "1261080964324876288"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Miami, Florida during May 2020, which was during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags like #floridaunemployment and #coronavirus, suggesting they are residing in Miami rather than just visiting. The mention of unemployment indicates they likely live there permanently. The tweet is about doing their eyebrows for a video call, which was a common activity during lockdown when people couldn't visit salons. There's no indication of travel plans or that they're temporarily in Miami. Given the pandemic context and unemployment reference, they are most likely a resident who will stay there for an extended period (more than 30 days).", "id": "1261124796558880769"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from someone who appears to be a construction professional or contractor working on a remodel project in west Seattle. The text indicates this is their own project (\"our west Seattle remodel\"), suggesting they are based in Seattle and working on a local renovation. Construction projects, especially remodels involving tile work, typically take weeks or months to complete. Given that this is posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 2020) with hashtags like #covidsaferemodels, it suggests this is ongoing professional work in the city where they're based, not a temporary visit. The person seems to be a local business (Crescent Builds) operating in Seattle, which indicates they will remain in the city well beyond 30 days.", "id": "1261127164650545160"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from an entertainment account based in Manhattan, NY promoting their music on YouTube with hashtags indicating they are a hip hop artist from Brooklyn/Queens/NYC. The hashtags #NYC and #NYCArtist suggest this is their home base, not a temporary visit. Additionally, the tweet was posted in May 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #Coronavirus hashtag), when travel was restricted and most people were staying in their home locations. The person appears to be a local artist promoting their work from their home city, suggesting they will remain in Manhattan/NYC for an extended period, well beyond 30 days.", "id": "1261210119280435200"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who lives in Kansas City, Missouri, not someone who is visiting. The hashtags used (#KCMOSortaOpen #KansasCity) suggest the person is a local commenting on the city's COVID-19 situation. The tweet was posted on May 15, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic, and the content (\"I know this shit ain't over\") along with the person wearing a face covering indicates they're dealing with ongoing pandemic restrictions. The person is expressing concern about others not wearing masks and emphasizing social distancing (#6feetawayorillpunchyouinthejunk). This strongly suggests they are a resident who will remain in Kansas City for an extended period (more than 30 days), not a visitor.", "id": "1261274952478740480"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet includes hashtags #workingduringpandemic and #covid19, and identifies the person as a #nursepractitioner in Santa Maria, California. This suggests that the person is not just visiting but working in Santa Maria as a healthcare professional during the pandemic. The image shows a work setup with a laptop and documents, indicating this is their regular workplace. Healthcare workers, especially nurse practitioners, typically have permanent or long-term positions in their communities, particularly during the COVID-19 pandemic when healthcare workers were essential and generally maintained their positions in one location. Therefore, the author is likely staying in Santa Maria for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1261335956130615298"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: There is no indication in the tweet that the user is traveling or temporarily visiting Sioux Falls. The tweet is about The Simpsons predicting the coronavirus back in 1993, not about any travel plans. The location tag (Sioux Falls, SD) and the casual nature of the post suggest this is likely the user's home or permanent residence. During the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the May 2020 date and coronavirus references), people were generally staying in their primary residences for extended periods. Without any mention of travel plans or temporary stay, it's reasonable to conclude the user will remain in Sioux Falls for more than 30 days.", "id": "1261384948792979456"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on May 15, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #covid, #covid19, and #quarentinefashion strongly suggest the person is under quarantine restrictions. The location tag shows Ukiah, California, which appears to be where the person resides rather than a travel destination. The person is wearing a face mask with a skull design, indicating they're following pandemic protocols. During this period, many people were under extended stay-at-home orders, especially in California which had strict lockdown measures. The casual, frustrated tone about COVID suggests this is their regular living situation rather than a temporary visit. Therefore, the person is likely staying in Ukiah for more than 30 days.", "id": "1261402777483264001"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 15, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and contains hashtags like #quarantine, #quarantinelife, and #covid19. The tweet is from Washington, DC, and based on the context, it appears the person is likely a resident of the city rather than a visitor. The image shows what seems to be a residential outdoor area with the person displaying a \"THANK YOU ESSENTIAL WORKERS\" sign, suggesting they are settled in this location. Given the pandemic circumstances at that time and the quarantine references, it's most likely the person would remain in Washington, DC for more than 30 days, as many people were staying in their home locations during lockdown periods.", "id": "1261418059153182728"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is about \"COVID-19 COOKING\" and was posted from Washington, DC on May 15, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The content suggests the person is cooking at home during lockdown rather than traveling. The hashtags focus on food preparation and culinary arts, indicating they are spending time cooking elaborate meals at home. There's no indication this is a temporary visit - rather it appears they are residing in Washington, DC during the pandemic lockdown period. Given the context of COVID-19 restrictions that were in place during this time, and the domestic cooking activity shown, it's most likely the person will remain in Washington for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1261442408534937601"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is posted from Manhattan, NY and contains hashtags like \"#oneyearanniversary\" and \"#coronavirus\". The tweet was posted on May 15, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic when New York City was under significant restrictions. The author appears to be a local resident commemorating something at \"Kettle of Fish\" (a bar in Manhattan). There's no indication of travel or temporary stay - rather, the content suggests the person lives in Manhattan. Given the pandemic context and that they're posting from their home city, it's most likely they would remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1261450174087528451"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Austin, TX on May 15, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. The image shows face masks with the Sutton Law Firm logo, and the user is expressing gratitude to this law firm for supporting a small business in making masks. Given that the user appears to be running or working with a small business in Austin that makes masks, and considering the tweet's location stamp matches the content, this suggests the person lives in Austin rather than visiting temporarily. The tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans or temporary stay. During the pandemic, especially in May 2020, most people were staying in their home locations for extended periods. Therefore, it's most likely the author will remain in Austin for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1261471951937298433"}, {"response": "Based on the image and tweet content, I can infer the likely duration of the person's stay in Lewes, Delaware.\n\nOption: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet uses hashtags like #stayhome and #stayhomestaysafe which suggest the person is residing at this location rather than visiting temporarily. The context shows they made their own mask during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 2020, early in the pandemic), indicating they are living in Lewes rather than just passing through. The fact that they're making personal protective equipment like a mask further suggests they are settled in this location for an extended period. Additionally, there's no indication of travel or vacation purposes in the tweet. Therefore, it's most likely the person will stay in Lewes for more than 30 days as they appear to be a resident.", "id": "1261500856198979586"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates the person has returned to work (likely at Costco, as tagged in the post) but is maintaining social distancing practices during the COVID-19 pandemic. The person is wearing a distinctive face mask and expressing a desire to stay away from others while at work. The hashtags #socialdistancing, #facemask, #stayaway, and #stayhome, along with the date of May 16, 2020 (during the pandemic), suggest they are following COVID protocols while working. Since they're already at work in West Homestead (as indicated by the location tag), they're likely a resident or regular employee there who will continue working for at least several days or weeks, but there's no indication of a permanent relocation or extremely long-term stay beyond a typical work period of 1-30 days.", "id": "1261657937199628289"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from the Capitol Hill Mansion Bed and Breakfast Inn account (@CAPHILLMANSION) showing what appears to be a chef working in their kitchen. The caption mentions \"Chef Life each morning at the @CAPHILLMANSION\" along with hashtags related to COVID-19 and social distancing. This suggests the person works at this bed and breakfast as a chef, preparing breakfast regularly. The image shows someone in chef attire with a mask (following COVID protocols) preparing food in a professional kitchen. This indicates a permanent or long-term employment situation rather than a temporary visit, especially considering this was posted during the pandemic when many hospitality workers continued their essential roles. Therefore, the person is likely to stay more than 30 days as they appear to be employed at this establishment in Denver.", "id": "1261671784778289153"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet indicates that the person is a business owner in Yorba Linda (with @luxeaesthetic1) who is planning to reopen their business after being closed during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #smallbusiness and #lovemyjob suggest this is their permanent business location, not a temporary visit. The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the early months of the pandemic when many businesses were closed indefinitely. Since they mention \"reopen (soon)\" and have used the closure time for \"rebranding\" and a \"touch up,\" this suggests they live and work in Yorba Linda and will remain there long-term to operate their business. This isn't a trip or temporary visit, but rather their place of residence and business.", "id": "1261676870275391490"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in May 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when New York City was under significant restrictions. The hashtags (#coronavirus, #covid19) and street art reference suggest the author is a local resident documenting pandemic-related street art in Tompkins Square Park in the East Village. The numerous neighborhood-specific hashtags (#eastvillage, #eastvillagenyc, #manhattan, #nyc) suggest this is their home area rather than a temporary visit. Given the pandemic context and that they appear to be documenting local street art, it's likely they were residing in Manhattan and would continue to stay there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1261693213376684033"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet describes a morning bike ride with family at the Las Vegas Strip, and the person is shown lifting their bike in front of the famous \"Welcome to Las Vegas\" sign. The tweet suggests this was a one-time activity (\"That was a #fun idea\") and expresses concern about catching COVID-19 from others who had the same idea of going for a bike ride. The casual nature of the activity, being described as a morning bike ride rather than as part of a vacation or longer stay, and the fact that they're at a tourist photo spot, suggests they're likely just there for a day trip or very short visit. The tweet was also posted from Paradise, NV (where the Las Vegas Strip is located), indicating they were already there when posting.", "id": "1261700357446656001"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet suggests that the person is likely a resident of Garland, TX, not a visitor. The evidence for this includes:\n\n1. The tweet is posted from Garland, TX (as shown in the location tag)\n2. The person mentions spending time with family \"last weekend\" while social distancing, indicating they live in the area\n3. The background shows a residential neighborhood setting\n4. There's no indication of travel or visiting - instead they're simply enjoying outdoor time with family while maintaining social distance during COVID-19\n5. The timestamp (May 16, 2020) was during the height of the pandemic when most people were staying in their home locations\n\nSince the person appears to be a resident rather than a visitor, they would likely stay in Garland for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1261712811375419393"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted from Los Angeles on May 16, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic. Several factors indicate the person is a resident of Los Angeles rather than a visitor:\n\n1. The hashtags include #coronavirus, #socialdistancing, #staysafe, and #stayhealthy, suggesting they are following pandemic protocols in their home city.\n\n2. The tweet shows a humorous image of extremely long \"social distancing shoes\" and mentions \"Finally! Now I am ready to walk,\" indicating they've been in lockdown or isolation and are now preparing to go outside with proper social distancing.\n\n3. The person is located in Brentwood, a residential neighborhood in Los Angeles, not typically a tourist area.\n\n4. The tone suggests they've been waiting for some time to be able to walk outside safely, consistent with being a resident experiencing lockdown restrictions rather than a visitor.\n\nGiven these contextual clues and the timing during the pandemic, it appears the person lives in Los Angeles and will remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days).", "id": "1261730272518815747"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 16, 2020, from Atlanta, GA, and refers to the last normal activity before COVID-19 shutdowns. The author mentions \"we shut down on March 15th\" and uses hashtags like #quarantinelife, #quarantine, and most tellingly #twomonthsgone. The tweet references a theater production at \"7 Stages\" (a theater in Atlanta) that was shut down due to COVID-19. Since the tweet was posted from Atlanta and the author appears to be a resident involved in local theater that shut down, combined with the pandemic context and the hashtag indicating they've already been in quarantine for two months, they likely remained in Atlanta for an extended period during lockdown (more than 30 days).", "id": "1261731625307701250"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, this appears to be a local activity during COVID-19 rather than a trip. The person is participating in a \"#socialdistancing group ride\" in Sacramento and mentions \"#bikepartysacramento\" which suggests they are a local resident participating in a community biking event. They're wearing a mask and following COVID protocols for an outdoor activity in their home city. The tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans or temporary visit, but rather shows someone engaging in a local recreational activity during the pandemic. Since they appear to be a resident of Sacramento, they would likely stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1261773998029000709"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 16, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags #covid-19 and #pandemic. The image shows a humorous sign in a bookstore noting that \"post-apocalyptical fiction\" has been moved to \"Current Affairs,\" making a joke about the pandemic situation. The user appears to be located in New Rochelle, New York, which was one of the early outbreak centers in the US. Given that this was during significant lockdown periods, and the tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans but rather seems to show the user is in their home location, it's most likely they would remain there for more than 30 days, especially considering the extended lockdown periods that were common during this phase of the pandemic.", "id": "1261798459058855945"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 16, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #stayhome, #washingtoniansathome, and #quarantine. The person refers to \"our neighborhood\" and is documenting their local area in Washington, DC as part of \"Coronavirus chronicles,\" suggesting they are a resident of the city rather than a visitor. The language and context indicate this is their home, not a temporary stay. Given the pandemic restrictions at that time and their apparent residence in the neighborhood, they will likely remain in Washington, DC for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1261805582417195008"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is about the author's mother graduating from college (Mount Saint Mary) with a BSN in nursing, during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 16, 2020). The author appears to be in Newburgh, NY to celebrate this graduation event. This suggests the author is likely visiting Newburgh for their mother's graduation ceremony and related celebrations. Such visits typically last for a few days to perhaps a couple of weeks, especially during the pandemic when travel was limited. There's no indication that the author lives permanently in Newburgh or is planning an extended stay beyond the graduation celebrations. Therefore, a stay of 1-30 days is most reasonable for this celebratory occasion.", "id": "1261833576489852939"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Branford, CT in May 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic as indicated by the hashtags #suckitcorona, #covid19, and #covid2020. The image shows what appears to be a family Easter egg hunt in their yard at Short Beach, Connecticut. This suggests the author lives in this area rather than visiting temporarily. The tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans or temporary stay. Given the pandemic context and the fact that the activity shown (Easter egg hunt) is taking place at what seems to be their residence, it's most likely the author is a resident who will remain in the area for an extended period, especially during COVID lockdowns that were common at that time.", "id": "1261988560334393344"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: There is no indication that the person is traveling or visiting Tonawanda temporarily. The tweet is about sharing an educational resource (a free eBook) regarding school reopening during COVID-19, suggesting the person is likely a resident or based in Tonawanda, NY. The professional nature of the tweet (promoting an educational resource about schools) and the fact that it was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic suggests the person is likely living or working in this location long-term rather than just visiting briefly. Therefore, the author will likely stay in Tonawanda for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1262181119765762048"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 17, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author refers to \"quarantine porchtrait\" and uses hashtags like #quarantine and #quarantinelife, indicating they are part of the community in Scottsdale, AZ (Valley of the Sun). The author appears to be a photographer offering porchtrait services to local families, suggesting they are a resident of the area rather than a visitor. Since they're offering ongoing photography services and are established enough to have a business phone number in the area, they likely live there permanently or for an extended period that would exceed 30 days.", "id": "1262191559753240576"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet shows spring pictures taken at Oakland University in Auburn Hills, MI, during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 17, 2020). The photographer appears to be affiliated with Oakland University (using hashtags like #oaklanduniversity and tagging the university). Given the pandemic context, the location (Auburn Hills, MI), and the person's apparent connection to the university, this suggests they are likely a resident or employee of the area rather than a visitor. During COVID-19, most university affiliates would have been staying in their primary location for extended periods. Therefore, the person would likely remain in Auburn Hills for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1262224369918660609"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the person is an instructor returning to the classroom during COVID-19 with safety precautions in place (mask and social dista