n 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Manhattan, NY in May 2020, which was during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #covid_19, #covid19, and #quarantine suggest the person is experiencing quarantine in New York City. The hashtags #newyorktough, #nyc, #ny, #newyorkcity, #newyork, #newyork_ig, #newyorklife, and #newyorker all strongly indicate that the person is a resident of New York, not just a visitor. Given that this was during a major lockdown period in NYC and the person appears to identify as a New Yorker, they are likely to stay in the city for an extended period (more than 30 days) rather than just passing through.", "id": "1262685883855187968"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be promoting a podcast episode that was recorded in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Given that the tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (May 19, 2020, as shown in the timestamp, with #coronavirus and #Covid19 hashtags), and it appears to be from a regular podcast (Episode 157), it suggests the person is likely based in Philadelphia rather than just visiting. The podcast seems to be an established series, and the person is likely a resident of Philadelphia who produces content there. During the pandemic, most content creators would be working from their home locations rather than traveling. Therefore, the stay in Philadelphia would likely exceed 30 days.", "id": "1262717492553637889"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Atlanta, GA on May 19, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The person is describing their daily state (feeling lazy, wearing gym clothes but not planning to work out) rather than mentioning any travel plans or temporary stay. The tweet includes the hashtag #corona, suggesting they're dealing with pandemic circumstances. The casual nature of the post and the comfortable home setting implied in the image suggest this is their residence rather than a temporary location. Given that the tweet was posted during pandemic lockdowns and there's no indication of travel plans, it's most likely the person is a resident of Atlanta who will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1262820248685772806"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted during May 2020, early in the COVID-19 pandemic when many businesses, especially personal services like hair salons, were shut down. The person is wearing a mask with \"WE'LL BE BACK\" printed on it, and the hashtags include #covid-19, #stayhome, and #hairdresser. The message \"We will be back - better than ever\" suggests this is a hairdresser or salon owner who has had to close their business during lockdown restrictions.\n\nGiven the timing (May 2020) when pandemic restrictions were extensive and long-lasting, especially for close-contact businesses like hair salons, and the fact that the person appears to be in San Francisco (where they likely live/work rather than visiting), they would likely be staying there for the extended duration of the pandemic restrictions, which would exceed 30 days for businesses like salons that were among the last to reopen.", "id": "1262830255649292292"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet says \"I'm back, baby!\" which suggests the person has returned to Iowa City, potentially to their home location. The hashtags include #theatre, #performanceart, and #thetether, indicating the person is involved in theater performance in Iowa City. During the COVID-19 pandemic (as noted by the #covid19 and #pandemic hashtags), this suggests they are likely returning to their home base or regular performance venue. The date of the tweet (May 19, 2020) was during the height of the pandemic when most people were staying in their primary residences. Additionally, the image shows what appears to be a residential backyard setting, further suggesting this is their home location rather than a temporary visit. Given these factors, it's most likely the person is planning to stay in Iowa City for an extended period (more than 30 days).", "id": "1262835229418573824"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Los Angeles on May 19, 2020, which was during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author includes the hashtag #covid and appears to be at a restaurant in Los Angeles (Barton G. The Restaurant). The image shows someone in what seems to be a restaurant setting with decorative food presentation. Since the person is at a restaurant in Los Angeles and using the location tag, it suggests they are likely a resident rather than a visitor. Given that this was during the height of the pandemic when travel was restricted and most people were staying in their primary residences, it's reasonable to conclude the author would remain in Los Angeles for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1262872068850360323"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet includes hashtags #stayhome, #quarantine, and most notably #100daysproject and #100daysproject2020, which suggests the author is participating in a creative project that spans 100 days. The author refers to \"Coronavirus Chronicles\" and is posting from Washington, DC during May 2020, at the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The colorful artistic image with \"Beyond the Senses\" text suggests this is part of a long-term creative endeavor during lockdown. Since the author identifies as a Washingtonian and is participating in a 100-day project, they are likely a resident who will remain in Washington for more than 30 days as they continue this pandemic project.", "id": "1262878293784694784"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from William Bender in San Jose, CA, and shows a team from local restaurants (jtownpizza, jacksbar, dipsomaniainc) who have completed COVID-19 reopening training. The context suggests these are local business owners and staff preparing for restaurant reopening during the pandemic. The tweet mentions owners who \"are working,\" indicating ongoing operations. Since this appears to be about local businesses where the leadership team works rather than a temporary visit, and the author is posting from San Jose, it's reasonable to infer that the author lives and works in San Jose and will stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1262943901901639686"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates this is a \"field trip\" and the person is \"back in the field\" at Hell's Kitchen, Manhattan. The hashtags #covid and #quarantine2020 suggest this is during the pandemic period. The person is wearing a mask and appears to be in some kind of industrial or technical facility (possibly related to their work). Since it's described as a \"field trip\" rather than a permanent relocation, and considering the pandemic context where travel was limited, it's most likely a work assignment that would last between 1-30 days. The excitement about being \"back in the field\" suggests it's a temporary work assignment rather than a very brief visit or a permanent move.", "id": "1263115654992924673"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: This tweet is from Mode 8 Yoga & Fitness, a fitness studio in Houston, and the message \"Welcome home, you lovely souls. Oh how we have missed you\" suggests they are reopening their facility after being closed during COVID-19 restrictions. The image shows their branded studio with a \"WELCOME HOME\" sign and rolled towels, typical of a yoga/fitness studio setting. The hashtags include multiple Houston area locations and fitness-related terms. Since this is their business location rather than a temporary visit, and they are welcoming clients back to their permanent establishment, they will likely remain there indefinitely (more than 30 days). This represents a reopening of their business, not a temporary visit.", "id": "1263208679295184896"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on May 20, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were widespread. The person is in West Hollywood and appears to be living there rather than visiting temporarily. The tweet has hashtags like #staysafe and #slaycoronavirus, suggesting they are following pandemic protocols at their residence. They mention \"#slay 'n another day in the #apocalypse,\" which indicates they've been experiencing the pandemic lockdown for some time and will continue to do so. Since this was during the height of COVID restrictions and the tweet doesn't mention any travel plans, it's reasonable to conclude they would remain in West Hollywood for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1263242547939475456"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on May 20, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author specifically mentions #Quarantine and #CoVid19 in her tweet, indicating she is in quarantine at her residence in Jersey City, NJ. During this period, lockdowns and stay-at-home orders were extensive, particularly in the New Jersey/New York area which was heavily impacted. The content of her tweet suggests she's finding creative ways to entertain herself during an extended quarantine period (dressing up pajamas with accessories), which was a common coping mechanism during long lockdowns. This suggests she will be staying in Jersey City for more than 30 days, as quarantine periods during this phase of the pandemic were often extended.", "id": "1263274704451833861"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on May 20, 2020, during the early months of the COVID-19 pandemic when stay-at-home orders were in effect in Los Angeles. The hashtags #quarantine, #quarantinelife, #covid19, and #pandemic indicate the author was experiencing the lockdown in Los Angeles. The tweet is from Tropico Roast