y visiting Costco for shopping. A typical Costco visit lasts a few hours at most, suggesting they would likely return home the same day.", "id": "1284145858926321664"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from an auctioneer (JJManningAuctioneers) announcing an upcoming auction of 4 estate lots in Carver, MA. The tweet mentions \"Coming Late Summer\" and includes hashtags related to land sales and real estate (#land #residential #absolute). The image shows a town sign for Carver, Massachusetts. Since the user is posting from Carver and appears to be an auctioneer or real estate professional operating in that area (announcing upcoming property sales there), they likely live or work in Carver permanently rather than just visiting. This suggests they will remain in Carver for an extended period beyond 30 days, as this appears to be related to their ongoing business operations in the area.", "id": "1284207232587124736"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a local resident of San Francisco rather than a visitor. The person is having lunch at a park near the Metreon center and mentions specific details about the restaurant location (Super Duper Burgers on Mission Street), suggesting familiarity with the area. The tweet was posted in July 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when people were practicing social distancing (as mentioned in the tweet). The casual nature of getting takeout food at a local burger place and eating it at a nearby park indicates routine local behavior rather than tourist activity. Therefore, it's most likely the person lives in San Francisco and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1284222994470957056"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (July 17, 2020) and includes hashtags like #pandemic and #stayinside, suggesting the author is at their home in Pasco, WA rather than traveling. The tweet mentions online courses and refers to her daughter demanding attention \"EVERY DAY during them all,\" indicating an ongoing situation. The hashtags #WashingtonState and #studentlife suggest this is where the author lives and is taking classes. The context indicates this is their permanent residence rather than a temporary stay, and they're dealing with the challenges of pandemic life, including online education and childcare. Therefore, the author will likely stay in Pasco for more than 30 days.", "id": "1284242915133358086"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions \"#quarantine\" and shows a cat looking upset or bored by the door, suggesting the author is in quarantine in Palm Springs. During the COVID-19 pandemic (the tweet is from July 17, 2020), typical quarantine periods lasted between 1-2 weeks, sometimes extending to 30 days, but rarely longer unless for specific health reasons. The use of hashtags like \"#freedom\" suggests the author is anticipating the end of their quarantine period. The fact that they're in Palm Springs rather than their home location also suggests this is a temporary situation rather than a permanent residence.", "id": "1284243280994205697"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in July 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The image shows a person using DJ equipment or turntables in what appears to be a home setup, and the hashtags include #quarantine, #ps4, and #nintendo, suggesting home-based activities. The person is also using hashtags related to hip-hop music production (#rap, #hiphop, #emcee, #undergroundhiphop), indicating they're likely creating music while in Miami. These factors, combined with the quarantine context of the pandemic, suggest this is not a short trip but rather that the person is residing in Miami for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1284252724171878402"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from Houston, TX in July 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic lockdown period. The content references \"Lockdown creativity\" and shows artwork in what appears to be a virtual exhibition at the Blaffer Art Museum (indicated by #myvirtualblaffer hashtag). This suggests the author is a local artist in Houston participating in a virtual exhibition during lockdown restrictions, not a visitor. The fact that the tweet originates from Houston and mentions lockdown creativity indicates the person lives in Houston and is likely to remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days) given the pandemic circumstances at that time.", "id": "1284274748084690951"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet suggests the person is from Chicago, as indicated by the location tag \"from Chicago, IL\" in the tweet metadata. The hashtags #RogersPark and #NorthSide further indicate they are referencing specific neighborhoods in Chicago. This appears to be someone posting about their local community rather than visiting temporarily. The tweet was posted during July 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, when people were generally staying in their home cities. The content is promoting mask-wearing in their local area, suggesting they are a resident advocating for community health safety measures. All these factors indicate the person likely lives in Chicago and would stay there for more than 30 days after posting.", "id": "1284297224701321222"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Laredo, TX in July 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The content of the tweet suggests the person is expressing frustration about COVID restrictions or perceptions, using hashtags related to entrepreneurship and business. There is no indication this is a temporary visit or trip. Instead, the tweet and location tag suggest the person is likely a resident of Laredo rather than a visitor. The person appears to be at home wearing a mask (with a Pittsburgh Steelers logo), which was common during the pandemic. Given these factors and the timing during the pandemic, it's most likely the person would remain in Laredo for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1284311090143334408"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Queens, NY during July 2020, which was during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The user is showing their PS4 game collection and mentioning how to \"stay sane in this pandemic,\" suggesting they are at home for an extended lockdown period. The hashtag \"#putamaskonit\" also indicates they're following pandemic protocols. Given that the user appears to be a resident (posting from Queens while referencing South Richmond Hill, both in New York), and considering this was during significant pandemic restrictions, it's likely they were staying at home for an extended period exceeding 30 days rather than making a short trip.", "id": "1284314057177051137"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in July 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The person is wearing a mask and using hashtags like #inthistogether, #covid19, #wearamask, and #socialdistancing, suggesting they are following pandemic guidelines. The tweet is posted from Dayton, Ohio, and there's no indication this is a temporary visit. Given the context of the pandemic and the location tag being a city rather than a specific tourist attraction, it appears the person likely lives in Dayton and will remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days) during the pandemic restrictions. The message encouraging others to \"stay safe\" suggests an ongoing situation rather than a brief visit.", "id": "1284492587030716416"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet appears to be from a business account called \"Super Tees Work\" promoting their t-shirt products. The account is based in Miami, FL (as indicated in the tweet location) and is advertising their merchandise through both virtual stores and Amazon. The hashtags include #miami, #florida, and other nearby locations (#palmbeach, #sunnyisles, #doral), suggesting this is a local business rather than someone visiting temporarily. The image shows a t-shirt product they are selling.\n\nSince this appears to be a local business account tweeting from their home base during the COVID-19 pandemic (note the #covid19 hashtag), they are likely established in Miami and will remain there indefinitely, certainly for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1284508735424073728"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on July 18, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author mentions needing \"a whole new wardrobe after #COVID,\" indicating they plan to stay in Dallas for the duration of the pandemic restrictions. The tweet also includes hashtags like #Bulking, suggesting they are working on a fitness transformation that typically takes months. The location tag shows they're at LA Fitness in Dallas, indicating they have a gym membership there. Given the pandemic context and their fitness goals, they appear to be a resident of Dallas rather than a visitor, suggesting they will stay more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1284529324973010948"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet and image, this appears to be a travel or fun adventure in New York, as indicated by hashtags like #travel, #adventure, #letsgo, and #NewYork. The person mentions \"finally find my light\" suggesting some sort of personal enjoyment or breakthrough during this trip. While the tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (July 18, 2020), the context seems to be about visiting friends and enjoying time in Manhattan rather than a permanent relocation. The hashtags clearly indicate travel purposes, and even during the pandemic, most tourist visits to cities like New York typically last from a few days to a couple of weeks. Therefore, a stay of 1-30 days is most likely for this type of travel experience.", "id": "1284530538368372737"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet shows a person at the Unisphere in Flushing Meadows-Corona Park in Queens, NY participating in what appears to be \"#justiceride6\" - a bicycle ride event organized by Street Riders NYC in honor of John Lewis and in support of the Black Lives Matter movement. The hashtags (#justiceforgeorge, #justiceforbreonna, #saytheirnames) suggest this is a specific protest or memorial ride event. Such organized rides are typically single-day events, and there's no indication that the author is staying in Queens beyond this specific activity. The tweet is documenting participation in this particular ride event, which would likely conclude within the same day.", "id": "1284595824224120832"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a local wine business in Portland, Oregon (as indicated by the location tag and hashtags like #buyportlandwine and #multnomahcounty). The business is promoting their Pineapple Mango Sangria that's available for the weekend. The hashtags #phase1 and #socialdistancing suggest this was during COVID-19 restrictions, specifically during Phase 1 of reopening. Since this appears to be a local business promoting their products rather than a visitor, and the tweet was posted during the pandemic (July 18, 2020), it's reasonable to conclude the author is a resident of Portland who will remain there for more than 30 days after posting.", "id": "1284604061392592896"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on July 19, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when Los Angeles was under significant lockdown restrictions. The author uses the hashtag #lockdown in their tweet, suggesting they are experiencing an extended stay at home. The embroidery project showing a coffin with \"home SWEET home\" has a humorous but resigned tone about being at home for a long period. The crafting activity (embroidery) is also consistent with longer-term home-based activities that became popular during pandemic lockdowns. Given the timing during the pandemic and the lockdown reference, it's likely the author is a resident of Los Angeles rather than a visitor, indicating they will stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1284729099282505728"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet and image suggest the person is at the beach in Long Branch, New Jersey for a day trip or brief outing. They're drinking Corona beer at the beach and joking about open container laws (with hashtags like #noopencontainers), which indicates this is a recreational beach day activity rather than a long-term stay. The context of being at the beach with alcohol suggests this is likely a single day outing or at most an overnight trip. There's no indication of accommodations or plans that would suggest a longer stay in the area.", "id": "1284843416040476678"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions \"#vacation\" and tags \"Disney's Vero Beach Resort,\" indicating the person is on vacation at a resort. The Goofy-themed face mask and Disney reference suggest they're enjoying a planned vacation stay. Most resort vacations typically last between a few days to a couple of weeks, falling within the 1-30 day range. The fact that this was posted during the pandemic (July 2020) suggests it's a vacation trip rather than a permanent relocation or very brief visit, as people were taking vacations despite COVID restrictions, but typically for standard vacation lengths.", "id": "1284854952326815748"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be about a day trip to Highlands in Charlottesville, Virginia. The hashtags #wearamask and #socialdistancing indicate COVID-era precautions