main in San Diego for more than 30 days.", "id": "1293943523000156161"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet explicitly states that the author \"spent some time wandering around San Francisco last week\" for a Washington Post assignment on pandemic fatigue. The use of past tense indicates that the activity has already been completed before the tweet was posted on August 13, 2020. Since the author mentions they were there \"last week\" and the assignment appears to be finished (with photos already taken), there's no indication they would remain in San Francisco after posting the tweet. The tweet suggests they were in San Francisco for a specific work assignment that has already concluded.", "id": "1293972980330971137"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, the author appears to be on a road trip to Maine as indicated by the hashtags \"#Roadtrip #Maine #Northeast.\" The image shows the person at what looks like a hotel or resort in South Portland, Maine. Road trips typically last for several days to a few weeks, but not usually longer than a month. Since the tweet specifically mentions \"Roadtrip\" and was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (August 13, 2020) when people were taking shorter vacations, it suggests the author is temporarily visiting Maine rather than relocating there permanently. Therefore, a stay of 1-30 days is most likely.", "id": "1293986539173818380"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet is promoting a fitness class (OUTDOOR FLY BOUNCE) that takes place in Brentwood, Tennessee. The image shows a group of people on mini trampolines wearing masks (during COVID-19 pandemic). The account \"@FreeBirdTribe\" appears to be a fitness business located in Brentwood, and they are advertising an upcoming class with limited spots available. The hashtags like #freebirdtribe and their promotion of classes suggests this is their permanent business location, not a temporary visit. Since they are running scheduled classes and operating as a local business, it's reasonable to conclude the author (business owner/operator) will stay in Brentwood for more than 30 days as this appears to be their established business location.", "id": "1294061721376755712"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions \"Covid dinner number, who's counting\" which strongly suggests this is one in a series of many meals prepared during an extended COVID lockdown period. The tweet was posted in August 2020, during the height of the pandemic when many people were under stay-at-home orders. The hashtag #DTSJ refers to Downtown San Jose, and the phrase \"I know the way\" suggests familiarity with the area, indicating the person likely lives in San Jose rather than visiting temporarily. The elaborate home-cooked meal with multiple courses (shrimp cocktail, roasted chicken, and homemade pie) also suggests someone who is settled in their location rather than traveling. Given these contextual clues during the COVID-19 pandemic, the author is likely staying in San Jose for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294082967418163203"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet is about changing a walk location for the day, suggesting this is a daily activity where the person tries different routes. The person mentions finding a \"gem\" at Harbor Bay Trail, and uses hashtags like #selfcare and #findingmetime, indicating this is a brief recreational activity. The content and tone suggest this is a single outing or day trip to enjoy nature and water views as part of their regular walking routine, not an extended stay in the city. There's no indication they're traveling or staying overnight in Alameda - they appear to be a local resident who simply changed their usual walking route for that particular day.", "id": "1294105249926406145"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: In the tweet, the person mentions \"Wearing this everyday\" referring to the mask, which suggests a long-term commitment to wearing it. The tweet was posted in August 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #actorslife and #thegoodlordbird (which appears to reference a TV show), along with mentions of @showtime and @blumhouse, suggest the person is likely working in Los Angeles as an actor or on a production. The tweet originates from Los Angeles, CA, and there's no indication this is a temporary visit. Given these factors and the context of the pandemic period when people were more likely to be stationed in one place for extended periods, it appears the person is living or working in Los Angeles for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1294109734962974721"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the person is living in Red Bank, not just visiting. Several key phrases support this: \"working from home\" suggests they are based there long-term, \"#emptynest\" implies they have a permanent residence there, and they're supporting \"#local #business\" which suggests they're a local resident themselves. Additionally, the tweet was posted in August 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when many people were working remotely from their permanent homes. The context suggests they are enjoying outdoor dining options in their hometown while working remotely, rather than making a temporary visit.", "id": "1294233499189338113"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet includes hashtags like #weekend, #weekendvibes, and #weekendmood, which strongly suggest this is a weekend getaway to Tillamook, OR. The image shows cocktails with an oceanfront view, and hashtags like #beachfront and #oceanfront indicate the person is enjoying a leisure trip. The post was made on a Friday (August 14, 2020) with \"Happy Friday!\" greeting, further supporting the weekend trip theory. While hashtags like #stayhome are included, these likely refer to the pandemic context of enjoying drinks at a vacation rental rather than going to public bars. A typical weekend trip would last 2-3 days, but it could potentially extend to a week or two vacation, placing this firmly in the 1-30 days range.", "id": "1294305482849607680"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a local resident enjoying outdoor activities at Runyon Canyon Park in Los Angeles, not a visitor or tourist. The tweet is dated August 14, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #staysafe and #wearamask, suggesting the person is following local health guidelines while exercising outdoors in their home area. The image shows someone out for a hike in what appears to be their regular exercise routine at a local park. There's no indication of travel or temporary stay in the caption - rather it suggests this is part of their normal life in Los Angeles. Therefore, the person is likely to remain in Los Angeles for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1294317010692775937"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet is a throwback (#tbt) to a 2014 youth retreat that took place in Atlanta, Georgia. The author is expressing that they \"can't wait to get back to this after #covid19,\" which indicates they are currently unable to hold such retreats due to the pandemic restrictions. Since this was posted in August 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and they're referencing waiting until after COVID-19 to resume such activities, this suggests a long-term waiting period rather than a short visit. The pandemic restrictions were extensive at that time, and normal activities like youth retreats were suspended indefinitely, likely for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294386057086025729"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the author is a college professor who is preparing for school to begin on Monday with online classes. The tweet was posted on August 14, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when many educational institutions were conducting classes remotely. The hashtags #zoomlife, #onlineschool, and #lausd (Los Angeles Unified School District) suggest that the author is based in Los Angeles and will be teaching remotely for the upcoming school term. Since college terms typically last several months, and the author appears to be a resident of Los Angeles (not a visitor), it's reasonable to conclude that they will remain in Los Angeles for more than 30 days as they teach through the academic term.", "id": "1294392983719223298"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet contains multiple real estate-related hashtags (#realtor, #househunting, #buyersagent, #sellersagent, #realestate) suggesting that the person is likely working in real estate in Sugar Land. Additionally, they mention \"New hair coming soon\" which indicates planning for the future in that location. The person appears to be a realtor based in Sugar Land rather than just visiting temporarily. The professional hashtags like #topproducer and #remaxhustle further suggest this is their work location. Therefore, they are likely to stay in Sugar Land for more than 30 days as it appears to be their place of residence and work.", "id": "1294412364326961152"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in August 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #stayhome, #quarantinelife, and #staysafe, which strongly suggest the person is living in Charlotte rather than just visiting. The tweet is about appreciating the beautiful sky from their location, which is consistent with someone finding joy in small things during extended lockdown periods. Since the tweet is geotagged at Charlotte, North Carolina, and contains pandemic-related hashtags indicating home quarantine, it's most likely the author is a resident who will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294425151891746821"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet includes hashtags \"#quarantine #quarantinelife #day4ofquarantine\" which clearly indicates the person is in quarantine and specifically on day 4 of their quarantine period. During the COVID-19 pandemic, typical quarantine periods lasted around 14 days, though they could vary. Since the person is explicitly on day 4 of quarantine in New Hope, Mississippi, they likely have approximately 10 more days remaining of their quarantine period. The total duration would fall within the 1-30 days range.", "id": "1294511928031707136"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe reason is that the tweet indicates this is a still from the author's music video titled \"Quarantine Freestyle\" which was created during \"a Summer of Covid-19\" as mentioned in the embedded YouTube description. The tweet was posted from Indianapolis, IN on August 15, 2020, and the content suggests the person is a local artist who created content during the pandemic quarantine period. Given that this appears to be their home base where they're producing music during the COVID-19 pandemic, and there's no indication of travel plans, they are likely staying in Indianapolis for an extended period (more than 30 days). The quarantine context further supports this conclusion as many people were under extended stay-at-home orders during this period.", "id": "1294684452032061441"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet includes hashtags related to the pandemic and quarantine (#pandemic #quarantine), and the image appears to be promoting a music release titled \"Pandemic Quarantine Drought\" dated 9-11-20. The tweet is from St. Louis, MO, and includes the hashtag #stl, suggesting this is the author's home location rather than a temporary visit. Since the tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (August 15, 2020) and references quarantine, it's likely the author is based in St. Louis for an extended period. Furthermore, the promotional nature of the content suggests the author is working on music in their home city during the pandemic restrictions, which typically lasted more than 30 days during this period.", "id": "1294691241419067393"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on August 15, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes the hashtag #quarantine. The image shows a cat sleeping on what appears to be a sweatshirt, and the tweet mentions Brooklyn and Williamsburg, indicating this is likely the person's home location rather than a temporary visit. The comfortable, domestic scene with a pet cat and personal items like a sweatshirt suggests the person lives there. Given the quarantine context during the pandemic and the domestic setting, it's reasonable to conclude the author would be staying in Brooklyn for more than 30 days, as this appears to be their residence.", "id": "1294712497723723777"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be a business announcement about \"Frankenstoned Masks\" being in stock at a store called \"myxedup\" with locations in Denver, Colorado Springs, and Pueblo. The user is tagging the store locations and using promotional hashtags. This suggests the person is likely a business owner or employee at this store in Colorado Springs, not a temporary visitor. The post was made during August 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and appears to be promoting merchandise (masks) from their regular business location. Therefore, the person is likely based in Colorado Springs and will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294751241646612482"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Santa Clarita, California on August 15, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags used (#besafe #wearamask) suggest the person is following pandemic safety protocols. There's no indication this is a temporary visit - it appears to be where they live, as they're commenting on the extreme heat (113\u00b0F shown on the car display with #TooF'inHot). The person seems to be in their car in their home city rather than traveling. During this period of the pandemic, most people were staying in their primary residences for extended periods. Given these factors and the lack of any travel context, it's most likely the author would remain in Santa Clarita for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294795478329176065"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nThe tweet suggests the author is enjoying a weekend getaway at Tillamook, OR. The key indicators are the hashtags #weekendvibes, #weekend, and #weekendmood, which imply a short vacation. The image shows a colorful cocktail with an ocean/beach view backdrop, matching the #beachfront and #oceanfront hashtags. The phrase \"It's my Friday!\" suggests they're starting their weekend, likely planning to stay for a few days. The post was made during August 2020 (during COVID), but they appear to be at a vacation spot rather than in quarantine at home (despite tags like #stayhome and #partyathome which were common during that period). This combination of factors suggests a weekend or short vacation stay of a few days, falling within the 1-30 day range.", "id": "1294797314826084352"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on August 15, 2020, which was during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags like #staysafeeveryone and #stayhome which were common during lockdown periods. The tweet shows a Cirkul water filter device that the person is using at home, suggesting they are set up for an extended stay. The location appears to be \"Tides on 17th\" in Phoenix, AZ, which sounds like a residential complex rather than a temporary accommodation. Given the pandemic context and the domestic nature of the content (showing home water filtration equipment), it's likely the author is a resident of Phoenix rather than a visitor, indicating they will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294798485057753090"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during August 2020, which was during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags #quarantinelife and #quarantine, suggesting they're living through an extended stay-at-home period. The tweet also mentions making homemade ice cream for family members, indicating they are at home (in Pflugerville, TX) and engaging in home-based activities. The post doesn't indicate this is a temporary trip or visit - rather, it suggests this is where they are residing during the quarantine period. Given the pandemic context and quarantine references, they are likely to stay in Pflugerville for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294822670303928321"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Los Angeles, CA, but the author is referencing Paddy's Pub, which is a fictional bar from the TV show \"It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia\" (as indicated by the hashtags). This suggests the author is making a humorous reference rather than actually visiting Philadelphia. The timestamp shows this was posted in August 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when quarantine measures were in effect. Since the tweet was posted from Los Angeles and there's no indication of travel plans, and given the pandemic context when many people were under extended stay-at-home orders, it's reasonable to conclude the author is likely a resident of Los Angeles who will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1294861056444006400"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from August 16, 2020, posted from Brooklyn, NY, and it appears the author is a local resident attending church there. The tweet mentions this is \"Heather's 1st time back to church since March,\" indicating they've been in the area for several months already during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags like #socialdistancing and #wearyour