n. The hashtags #socialdistancing and #MaskOn suggest this was a single event held with COVID precautions. There's no indication of an extended stay in Trenton - it reads as someone who conducted or participated in a one-day workshop and is sharing the completed art piece afterward, likely leaving soon or already having left.", "id": "1295861503200043010"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Los Angeles in August 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #covid19 hashtag). The person is discussing self-tape auditions becoming something positive to do rather than a burden. The tweet suggests they are an actor/actress (based on hashtags #actor #acting #actress) likely living in Los Angeles (a major entertainment hub) rather than just visiting. The mention of finding activities to do during COVID also implies they are settled there for an extended period. Additionally, the tweet is framed as a reflection on a lifestyle change rather than a temporary situation, suggesting the person is based in Los Angeles rather than visiting briefly.", "id": "1295868719919505413"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet is about school reopening plans during the COVID-19 pandemic, using hashtags #coronavirus, #covid19, #backtoschool, and #remotelearning. The image shows what appears to be a child's chaotic crayon drawing, which the author is using to humorously represent the disorganized nature of school reopening plans. \n\nSince the tweet mentions Bradley, Illinois and includes education-related hashtags, it suggests the author is likely a resident, teacher, or parent in that location. Given that this was posted in August 2020 (during the height of the pandemic) and relates to school plans for the upcoming academic year, the author is likely established in Bradley rather than just visiting. School years typically last several months, and during COVID-19, many people remained in their home communities for extended periods due to restrictions. Therefore, the author would likely stay in Bradley for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1295868836969947138"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is promoting merchandise (t-shirts and shorts) from what appears to be the author's own brand (\"@LUCHIMILLIANI\" promoting \"@LUCHIMILLIANI\" products). The fact that they are tagging Atlanta with an @ symbol suggests this is their location rather than a temporary visit. Additionally, the tweet mentions their \"WEBSITE\" and \"Link in bio\" indicating they're running a business based in Atlanta. The promotional nature of the content and the pandemic context (mentioning \"2020 PANDEMIC TEE\") suggests the person is not just visiting but is based in Atlanta for their business operations. Therefore, they're likely to stay in Atlanta for more than 30 days.", "id": "1295915942921175047"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from Habitat for Humanity CFI (@HabitatCFI) in New Albany, IN, and shows a construction site where volunteers are working on a home building project. The account appears to be based in New Albany as indicated by the tweet location. This isn't a temporary visit but rather the organization's home base where they conduct their ongoing home building activities. The tweet is thanking volunteers who came to help with construction, suggesting that Habitat for Humanity CFI is the permanent organization in this location running the #WomenBuild project. Given that they are based in New Albany and managing construction projects there, they would likely remain in the city for more than 30 days as they continue their mission of building homes.", "id": "1296134214086270982"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: There's nothing in the tweet that indicates the author is planning to leave Windsor, CA anytime soon. The tweet is expressing disappointment about the Antarctica season being canceled until January 2021 due to COVID-19, suggesting the author has #PolarFever (enthusiasm for Antarctic travel) but cannot pursue this interest currently. Since the tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (August 19, 2020) and the author is lamenting travel restrictions, it's likely they're residing in their home location of Windsor rather than temporarily visiting. Given the pandemic circumstances and no mention of travel plans, the author is likely to remain in Windsor for more than 30 days.", "id": "1296173459056349185"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet appears to be a business advertisement from a company called \"SHOCK & AWE\" (@shockandawepro) promoting their stage rental services in Atlanta. The image shows a stage setup with musical equipment including drums, microphones, and speakers. The hashtags #stagerentals and #shockandaweproductions suggest this is their business location rather than a temporary visit. Since this is a business operating in Atlanta (as indicated by #atlantaeventrentals and #atlantaconcert), and the tweet is promoting their services, it's reasonable to conclude that the author (business owner) is based in Atlanta and will stay there for an extended period (more than 30 days) to run their business operations.", "id": "1296223836753600514"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet describes a \"field trip\" to the McWane Science Center in Birmingham, which is typically a day activity. The author mentions it was \"today\" with \"quarantine buddies\" and uses hashtags like #stillsocialdistancing and #wearamask, indicating this was a special outing during the pandemic rather than a long-term stay. The phrase \"Best field trip EVER\" suggests this was a single-day excursion to the science center. Field trips are typically day activities that don't involve overnight stays, so the author likely returned home the same day after visiting the science center.", "id": "1296243828102397955"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet states \"Delivered another black Cali to Brooklyn today,\" indicating that the person was in Brooklyn for a delivery. The use of the word \"delivered\" suggests a quick drop-off activity rather than an extended stay. The image shows what appears to be a black motorcycle (likely the \"black Cali\" mentioned) parked on a Brooklyn street. This suggests the person made a delivery trip to Brooklyn and would likely return after completing the delivery. There's no indication of the person planning to stay in Brooklyn, and the business-like nature of the delivery suggests they would likely leave the same day.", "id": "1296252923945443329"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet contains several hashtags that suggest the person is a local resident of Chicago rather than a visitor, including #chitownlove and #chitown (nickname for Chicago). The tweet was posted in August 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #pandemic hashtag). The person is wearing a mask in the image and appears to be taking a selfie with street art in the Pilsen neighborhood of Chicago. There's no indication of travel or visiting - instead, the content suggests someone who lives in Chicago and is enjoying local street art. During this pandemic period, people were generally staying in their home cities for extended periods, especially if they were residents. Therefore, it's most likely the person will remain in Chicago for more than 30 days.", "id": "1296273115803074560"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during August 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #covid19 and #quarantine. The person appears to be getting their hair done at Astor Place Hairstylists in Manhattan. The context suggests this is likely their home city rather than a trip, as they're doing routine activities like getting a haircut while wearing a mask during the pandemic. The hashtags and timing strongly indicate they are a resident of Manhattan who went out for a haircut during quarantine, not a visitor. Therefore, they will likely stay in Manhattan for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1296287069505978369"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet doesn't indicate this is a temporary visit or trip to Atlanta. Instead, the content suggests the person is sharing everyday life moments (referencing Wednesday and using hashtags like #wcw - Woman Crush Wednesday). The geotag of Atlanta, Georgia appears to be their regular location rather than a travel destination. Additionally, the tweet was posted in August 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, when most people were staying in their home cities for extended periods. The personal style of the tweet and mention of makeup products (Lipgear) suggests this is part of their regular life in Atlanta rather than a temporary visit.", "id": "1296290984498790402"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Toledo, OH in August 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and appears to show the person in what looks like a home podcasting setup. The hashtags include #covid and many podcast-related tags, suggesting this is an ongoing activity they're engaged in from their residence. The professional microphone setup with an articulating arm indicates a permanent or semi-permanent arrangement. Given that this was during a period of extended lockdowns and the tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans or temporary visit, it's most likely the person lives in Toledo and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting.", "id": "1296375731254173696"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet is about the first day of school, specifically mentioning \"#blendedschool\", \"#remotedesk\", and \"#backtoschool\" along with location tags for Westerville. The image shows students at home with lap