reparing for a lengthy stay at home rather than a short trip. Therefore, the author is likely staying in Baltimore for more than 30 days.", "id": "1301776445694529537"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on September 4, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The user mentions this is a \"#covid19 edition\" of their \"#athomedj\" series, which suggests they are at home in Cape Girardeau, MO, not traveling. The hashtags and content indicate this is their regular residence where they're sharing music suggestions during lockdown. The tweet doesn't indicate any travel plans or temporary stay - it appears to be content created by someone at their home location during pandemic restrictions. Given the timing during COVID-19 when many people were staying at home for extended periods, and that this appears to be their home city, they would likely remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1301790712321712128"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on September 4, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The person refers to the \"#covid19 edition of the #athomedj sharing suggestions\" and uses hashtags like \"#athomedj,\" suggesting they are staying at home for an extended period due to the pandemic. There's no indication this is a temporary trip or visit to Cape Girardeau, MO (where the tweet was posted from). Rather, it appears they are sharing music they're listening to during what was likely an extended stay at home during pandemic restrictions. Given the context of COVID-19 lockdowns and the at-home nature of their activities, they are likely staying for more than 30 days.", "id": "1301804355188740097"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Fort Lauderdale, FL on September 4, 2020, and the content suggests the author is a local baker/cookie decorator who created these decorated cookies for a baby shower event. The tweet indicates this was \"the first large special event after the lockdown was lifted,\" which suggests the author is based in Fort Lauderdale rather than visiting temporarily. The professional nature of the cookies (with detailed decorations for a baby shower) further suggests this is their place of business/residence. There's no indication of travel or visiting - instead, this appears to be someone who lives and works in Fort Lauderdale who is resuming their normal business activities after COVID restrictions were eased.", "id": "1301869978283659265"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, there is no indication that the author is traveling or visiting Cape Girardeau temporarily. The tweet was posted during September 2020, which was during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags like #covid19 and #athomedj, suggesting they are at home during this period. The reference to being an \"at home DJ\" and sharing music during COVID implies this is their regular residence rather than a temporary visit. Additionally, the tweet doesn't mention any travel plans or indicate they are visiting Cape Girardeau. Therefore, it's most likely that the author lives in Cape Girardeau and will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1301888925330538496"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet includes the hashtags #nycliving and #nycdiaries which strongly suggest the person lives in New York City rather than just visiting. Additionally, the tweet mentions \"Mom on duty during this pandemic\" and shows a parent taking a child for a stroll in Central Park, indicating this is their regular life during the pandemic. The hashtag #nycduringcorona further suggests they are residing in NYC during the COVID-19 pandemic. Since they appear to be residents of Manhattan/NYC rather than tourists, they would likely stay more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1301895437864099852"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and the author appears to be living in San Francisco rather than just visiting. Several clues indicate this is a long-term stay: 1) The hashtags mention \"#covid #aftereffects\" and the feeling that all days blend together (#blended #daze), which suggests experiencing pandemic lockdown life; 2) The person is posting from San Francisco as their location, not as a visitor; 3) The motivational sign in the image about \"Relax. We're all crazy\" suggests coping with ongoing pandemic stress; 4) There's no indication of travel or temporary visit. This appears to be someone experiencing the pandemic blur of days in their home city, suggesting they will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1301900299603292161"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Cape Girardeau, MO on September 4, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The user mentions this is a \"#covid19 edition\" of their \"#athomedj\" music sharing, suggesting they are at home during the pandemic. The hashtags and content indicate this is part of their regular life rather than a temporary visit. Since this appears to be their home location during COVID-19 restrictions and not a trip, they are likely staying there for an extended period (more than 30 days). Additionally, there's no indication of travel plans or that Cape Girardeau is a temporary location for them.", "id": "1301906811801546755"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet mentions \"#DistanceLearning\" and \"#Homeschool gig\" which indicates that the person is involved in remote education activities in Phoenix. The tweet was posted on September 4, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when many schools were operating remotely. The person seems to be settled in Phoenix with children who are attending school there, celebrating a half-birthday for \"Miss Stella,\" and referring to the homeschooling situation as a \"gig\" that \"ain't so bad after all.\" The image shows children enjoying donuts during a break from learning activities. This strongly suggests the author is living in Phoenix rather than just visiting, and will likely stay for more than 30 days as they appear to be residents engaged in ongoing distance learning during the pandemic.", "id": "1301913028661858307"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet is wishing people \"Happy Aloha Friday\" and saying \"Have a blessed long weekend Everyone!!\" which indicates this is a work-related visit on a Friday before a weekend. The hashtags #goarmy and #goarmyhawaii along with the Army Recruiter title suggest this is related to their job. They are encouraging people to \"Stop by to see one of our awesome educators,\" indicating they are at a temporary recruitment or educational event. The image shows people in what appears to be an educational or office setting. Since they're specifically mentioning the \"long weekend,\" it suggests they'll be leaving after this Friday workday or event.", "id": "1301978663458541568"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet shows that the author is at a movie theater in Herndon, VA for a \"date night\" watching \"New Mutants.\" This indicates they are just visiting the theater for the duration of the movie (typically 2-3 hours). The hashtag #datenight suggests this is a single evening activity. There's no indication they are staying in Herndon beyond the movie showing - they appear to be local residents who are just out for an evening at the movies and will likely return home after the film ends. This is a typical short-duration activity rather than a prolonged stay in the city.", "id": "1301980432712556544"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet and promotional image, the author is advertising an outdoor market event in Brooklyn that will take place \"tomorrow\" (September 5, 2020) from 12-6pm. The tweet was posted on September 4, 2020 from Manhattan, and they're promoting a one-day event in Brooklyn. The event itself only lasts 6 hours (12-6pm), and there's no indication they'll be staying in Brooklyn beyond this specific event. This suggests the author will be in Brooklyn for less than 24 hours - just long enough to run the outdoor market event and then presumably return.", "id": "1302014069927563266"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is wishing people a \"Happy Labor Day Weekend\" and is dated September 4, 2020. Labor Day weekend is typically a 3-day holiday weekend in the US. The author appears to be in Austin, Texas for this holiday period. While there's construction material (bricks, shovel, wood) visible in the image suggesting some kind of project, the specific mention of the Labor Day Weekend indicates this is likely a short-term visit or a brief holiday stay. The seasonal nature of the post and lack of indication of permanent residence suggests the author would likely stay in Austin for the duration of the holiday weekend and perhaps a bit longer, but not for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1302022973377789954"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet appears to show a person in a gym in Chula Vista, CA, taking a selfie while wearing workout clothes and a face mask (the \"new gym norm\" as mentioned in the tweet). The date of the post (September 4, 2020) is during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags and context suggest this is their regular gym, not a temporary vacation spot. The person is likely a resident of Chula Vista rather than a visitor, indicated by their familiarity with the gym and reference to it as a \"norm\" for them. Since this appears to be their home city where they regularly work out, they would be staying there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302050724029902848"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who lives in Minneapolis, not someone who is visiting. They're attending a \"socially distanced social in the park\" with what seems to be their regular social group (Spirit Garage, which appears to be a local community). The tweet was posted during September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and mentions their \"new #spiritgarage #masks\" suggesting they are part of this local community. The casual nature of the gathering in a local park and visiting a local restaurant (@allsquarempls) indicates this is their home city rather than a temporary visit. Therefore, the author is likely to stay in Minneapolis for more than 30 days as they appear to be a resident.", "id": "1302061535997632515"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet is thanking Heartland Optical for an eye appointment for two people (Talley and Liem), and the images show what appears to be a routine eye examination at an optical clinic. The appointment seems to be a standard eye check-up with possible glasses/contacts fitting as indicated by the hashtags #eyeballs #glasses #contacts. This type of medical appointment typically lasts for 1-2 hours at most, after which the person would likely return home. There's no indication in the tweet that suggests the author is staying in Lincoln for an extended period - it appears they just visited the city specifically for this appointment. Therefore, their stay in Lincoln after posting the tweet would likely be less than 24 hours.", "id": "1302106153673994240"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet describes a \"quickie selfie from yesterday's photoshoot\" supporting a voting rights initiative called \"thevotemask\" in Brookline Village. The post includes hashtags like #vote2020, suggesting this is related to the 2020 election campaign activities. This appears to be a specific event or campaign photoshoot that the person participated in, rather than indicating they are a permanent resident or just passing through briefly. Campaign activities typically last for several days t