ap between the current date (September) and winter (December/January), suggesting they will continue working from home for several months. The fact that they're promoting a microwave bowl set for warm soup during winter office lunches further supports that they anticipate being in this location through the winter season. Given this context and the timing during the pandemic when many were working remotely for extended periods, the author will likely stay in Sapulpa for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302247725459742722"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on September 5, 2020, from Queens, NY during the COVID-19 pandemic. The user is talking about barbecuing during the pandemic and appears to be in a local park or outdoor space. The hashtags include #nyc, #newyorkcity, #brooklyn, and #queens, suggesting the person is a local resident rather than a visitor. The image shows someone grilling outdoors, which is a typical activity for residents. Given the pandemic context, the numerous local hashtags, and no indication of travel, it's likely the person lives in Queens and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1302263777153474560"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet describes a meetup specifically for pie at a place called 29 Diner. The phrasing \"Meetup for PIE!\" and hashtags like \"#bestiesdate\" suggest this is a brief, casual outing with friends to enjoy pie at a diner they had missed visiting. The tweet also includes \"#coronavirus\" and \"#selfisolation\" indicating this is during pandemic restrictions, but they're briefly breaking isolation for this specific food outing. The nature of visiting a diner for pie typically implies a short visit of a few hours at most, not an overnight or extended stay. Therefore, the author is likely staying in Fairfax for less than 24 hours for this specific pie meetup.", "id": "1302295667214290946"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from a person who appears to be associated with Zoombie Nation Tattoo Studio in Fresno, CA, as indicated by the location tag. The images show watercolor pirate-themed artwork, which suggests this is their workplace where they create tattoo designs. The hashtags like #zoombienation and mentioning the tattoo studio indicates this is likely their regular place of employment rather than a temporary visit. Artists typically work at tattoo studios for extended periods, and since this appears to be their business/workplace in their home city, they would likely stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302301882619236352"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates this is a morning swimming session at a local pool in Rancho Santa Margarita. The person mentions going \"up early for #saturdaymorning #swimming before it gets crowded\" and accomplishing a 5000m swim. This suggests a routine activity rather than a trip or vacation. The hashtag #fitover50 and #oldswimmersjustkeepgoing suggest this is part of the person's regular fitness routine in their local area. There's no indication they're visiting or staying in Rancho Santa Margarita - it appears to be their local swimming spot that they're using for a few hours on a Saturday morning before returning home.", "id": "1302302114908168192"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates the person is at IKEA (tagged in the post) in Stoughton, MA, which is a retail store where people typically shop for a short period of time. The image shows the person's feet standing on what appears to be the floor of the store with a yellow line, likely related to social distancing measures. The hashtags #covid, #socialdistancing, #6feet, #goaway, and #keepyourdistance suggest this is a brief shopping trip during the pandemic where the person is emphasizing the need for others to maintain distance. Shopping trips to IKEA, even lengthy ones, typically last only a few hours, not overnight or multiple days.", "id": "1302317571258097665"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet suggests that the person is a local resident of Brooklyn, not just a visitor. The person says \"Your girl is back!!!\" indicating a return to her home area rather than a temporary visit. The tweet also references \"Bedford-Stuyvesant\" which is a neighborhood in Brooklyn, suggesting familiarity with the area. Additionally, the hashtag \"#BrooklynSaturdays\" implies this is a regular occurrence rather than a one-time visit. The tweet was posted during Labor Day weekend in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, when people were mostly staying in their home locations. The emphasis on safety with hashtags like \"#socializesafely\" and \"#wearamask\" further suggests the person is a resident navigating daily life during the pandemic rather than a tourist.", "id": "1302333518828187648"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, the photographer appears to be a resident of Los Angeles conducting a \"#pandemicportrait series\" at Silver Lake (which is a neighborhood in Los Angeles). The hashtags #laartist and #lalawyer suggest this person is a local artist in LA. Additionally, the post is from September 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic when most people were staying in their home cities. There's no indication of travel or temporary visit - rather this seems to be an ongoing portrait project the photographer is conducting in their home city. Therefore, they would likely stay in Los Angeles for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1302369530606280704"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (September 5, 2020) and includes hashtags like #covid19 and #athomedj. The person is sharing music from their shuffle as part of what appears to be an ongoing at-home DJ activity during the pandemic. There's no indication this is a trip or temporary visit to Cape Girardeau, MO. Instead, it seems they are likely residing there during the pandemic lockdown period, sharing music as a regular activity while staying at home. The context suggests this is their place of residence rather than a temporary location, so they would likely stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302383101876805635"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions Franklin Square in Philadelphia with hashtags like #philly and #philadelphiavibes, indicating the person is in Philadelphia. The image shows a carousel in what appears to be Franklin Square park. The casual nature of the visit (noting it \"wasn't too crowded\" on a \"Saturday afternoon\") suggests this is either a local outing or a short-term visit to the city. Given the COVID-19 context (with the #covid19 hashtag) mentioned in the tweet from September 2020, it's unlikely this represents a very brief visit (less than 24 hours) or an extremely long stay (more than 30 days). The tweet suggests a leisure visit to a local attraction during the pandemic, typical of someone spending several days to a few weeks in the city.", "id": "1302399101456330754"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on September 5, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author refers to sharing music as part of an \"#athomedj\" series specifically labeled as the \"#covid19 edition,\" suggesting they are regularly at home sharing music during this time. The location (Cape Girardeau, MO) appears to be their residence rather than a travel destination, as they're posting about regular at-home activities (listening to their music shuffle) rather than tourism. Given the pandemic context, the hashtags related to being at home, and no indication of travel plans, it's most likely the person is a resident of Cape Girardeau who will remain there for an extended period (more than 30 days).", "id": "1302399762260545539"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Seattle, WA and the person is visiting the Woodland Park Zoo, which is located in Seattle. The tweet mentions \"covid restrictions\" at the zoo and includes hashtags suggesting a local experience. The image shows what appears to be a child looking at grizzly bears in an exhibit. This suggests the person is a local resident of Seattle rather than a tourist, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic when travel was restricted. The fact that they're visiting a local zoo and are familiar enough with it to comment on the COVID restrictions indicates they likely live in Seattle and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1302429293805408256"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions staying at an Airbnb in Pasadena (\"Pasadena Posh 2 Airbnb\"), which suggests a temporary stay rather than permanent residence. The mention of Labor Day weekend indicates the person is there for the holiday weekend, which typically lasts 3-4 days. Airbnb stays are generally short-term accommodations ranging from a few days to a few weeks. The tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #covid19 hashtag), but since this appears to be a holiday trip rather than a quarantine situation, and given that it's specifically at an Airbnb for Labor Day weekend, the duration is most likely between 1-30 days.", "id": "1302436627223060485"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet describes a visit to Seattle as \"the first time since Covid 19 appeared\" and mentions that \"Most of her time was spent outside in a mask,\" suggesting a temporary trip to the city. This language implies the person is visiting Seattle rather than relocating there permanently. The post shows the person visiting a little free library in the Ballard neighborhood of Seattle, which appears to be a tourist or short-term visitor activity. Given this was during the pandemic (September 2020), it's likely a brief trip rather than a long-term stay, but longer than just a day trip since they're exploring different neighborhoods. This suggests a typical vacation or short-term visit lasting between 1-30 days.", "id": "1302450451229618176"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted from Downtown Albuquerque with hashtags like #downtownabq, #burquestrong, and #igersabq, which suggest the author is a local resident of Albuquerque rather than a visitor. The use of hashtags like #blockparty, #streetviews, and #nighlife indicates participation in local community activities. Additionally, the tweet was posted during September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic (as indicated by the #covid19 hashtag), when most people were staying in their home cities. The casual nature of the post and familiarity with local areas (4th street) further suggests this is someone who lives in Albuquerque and will remain there for more than 30 days after posting.", "id": "1302469570557546496"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on September 6, 2020, and contains several indicators of a long-term stay during the pandemic. The hashtags #quarentinelife, #march2020, and #virusmonththree suggest the person has been collecting these corks since at least March 2020 (when many lockdowns began). The large collection of wine and champagne corks displayed in the image further supports a lengthy stay, as it would take considerable time to accumulate this many. The phrase \"pandemic times\" and reference to \"month three\" of the virus indicates an ongoing situation. Since the tweet was posted during COVID-19 when extended lockdowns were common, and there's no indication this is a trip, it's reasonable to conclude the author will remain at this location for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302559682901536771"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet shows the person taking a photo of a COVID-related poster at a closed movie theater in Vineyard Haven, Martha's Vineyard. There's nothing in the tweet indicating an extended stay - they appear to be simply documenting the poster while passing by. The post is tagged with location information and appears to be casual observation of pandemic-related signage rather than indicating a long-term visit or residence. Without any mention of staying, lodging, or extended plans in the area, it seems most likely this is just a brief visit or the person is a local briefly passing by the theater.", "id": "1302568839956365312"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on September 6, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and the location appears to be in Philadelphia with specific street references (Larchwood & 49th @ Spruce). The hashtags used (#sequester, #coronaviruspandemic, #bettersafethansorry) suggest the person is living in Philadelphia rather than visiting. The tweet shows they're documenting abandoned masks in their neighborhood, which indicates an ongoing observation rather than a short visit. This suggests the person lives in Philadelphia and will stay there for more than 30 days. The specific mention of a \"series\" of abandoned mask sightings also indicates they expect to be in the area for an extended period.", "id": "1302631825987100672"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet and image show a person donating convalescent plasma at an American Red Cross facility in Philadelphia. This is a one-time medical procedure that typically takes 1-2 hours to complete. The person is mentioning this as a positive outcome of having recovered from COVID-19, but there's no indication they are staying in Philadelphia beyond this donation appointment. Plasma donation is an outpatient procedure that doesn't require an overnight stay, so they would likely return home the same day after completing the donation process.", "id": "1302655450303823872"}, {"response": "Based on the tweet and image, I can infer the following:\n\nOption: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet contains several relevant hashtags including #kitchenquarantine, #southflorida, #covid19, and #Miami. The post was made on September 6, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtag #kitchenquarantine specifically suggests the person is spending an extended period at home baking during quarantine. The location appears to be in Hallandale Beach, FL (near Miami in South Florida), which seems to be where the person resides rather than a place they're visiting briefly. The elaborate homemade muffins also suggest someone who has settled in for an extended stay. Given the pandemic context and quarantine reference, it's most likely the person will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302656532090363906"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The image shows people enjoying time at Mission Dolores Park in San Francisco, and the tweet uses hashtags like #California, #SanFrancisco, #SF, #TheBay, #Hella, and #HellaBay, which suggest a strong local connection rather than temporary tourism. The tweet was posted on September 6, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and the casual nature of the gathering at a local park indicates the author is likely a resident of San Francisco rather than a visitor. The comfortable familiarity with local terminology (#HellaBay is local slang) further supports that the author lives in the area and will stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1302714228806221825"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet includes the hashtag #quarantine, which during the COVID-19 pandemic (this was posted in September 2020) typically indicated an extended stay due to lockdown or isolation requirements. The tweet also mentions \"working hard over here\" and is geotagged at Baltimore, Maryland, suggesting the person is based there rather than just visiting. The phrase \"I can't wait to see you, again\" implies the person is separated from someone for an extended period due to quarantine restrictions. Given the context of the pandemic in September 2020 when quarantine periods were often lengthy, and the indication that the person appears to be living and working in Baltimore, the stay would likely be more than 30 days.", "id": "1302714928068001798"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: Based on the tweet, this appears to be a repost of someone else's aerial photo of Corona Del Mar/Newport Beach, rather than indicating the author is physically visiting the location. The hashtag \"#flyover\" suggests this is an aerial photograph, and the attribution \"From RJ roej32\" indicates the author is sharing someone else's content. There's no indication in the tweet that the author is actually staying in Newport Beach beyond the time needed to share this appreciation post. The tweet seems to be simply appreciating the beauty of the area rather than documenting a stay or visit.", "id": "1302750509388959744"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: In the tweet, the person explicitly states \"Home for the night\" and mentions \"heading back home tomorrow.\" They describe their trip as \"Quick getaways for the weekend visit to the SD wine county.\" The image shows a hotel room at what appears to be the Andaz San Diego (as indicated by the hashtag #andazsandiego). This clearly indicates they are staying in San Diego for just one night and will be leaving the following day, which is less than 24 hours from when the tweet was posted.", "id": "1302763795543207936"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe reason is that the tweet mentions \"Tabatha's Manic Quarantine Kitchen,\" which strongly suggests the author is in quarantine due to COVID-19. The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the pandemic when many people were under extended lockdowns. The phrase \"Quarantine Kitchen\" implies ongoing cooking activities during an extended stay at home. Additionally, the tweet is geotagged from Los Angeles, California, indicating this is likely the author's residence rather than a temporary visit. The hashtag \"#sundaydinner\" suggests routine meal preparation at home. Given these contextual clues and the timing during the COVID-19 pandemic, the author is likely staying in Los Angeles for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1302785651629318150"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the person is observing outdoor dining in San Francisco's Mission District, and the hashtags (#sanfrancisco, #excelagent) suggest they are likely a local real estate agent or resident sharing observations about their city during the pandemic. The tweet doesn't mention visiting or traveling, but rather documents a change in the city (widespread outdoor dining) due to the coronavirus pandemic. The fact they're posting from San Francisco and using location tags suggests this is their home base, not a temporary visit. Therefore, they are likely to stay in San Francisco for more than 30 days as a resident.", "id": "1302826852076187649"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Orlando, FL in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags like \"#pandemic\" and phrases like \"All we do these days is celebrate life,\" suggesting an ongoing situation rather than a brief visit. The location tag \"The Yard at Ivanhoe\" (a venue in Orlando) and the casual social gathering shown in the image with friends enjoying themselves with glitter and festive elements suggest that the author is likely living in Orlando rather than just visiting. This appears to be their local social scene during the pandemic period, indicating they will remain in Orlando for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1302876836939149312"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a local photographer in Brooklyn, as indicated by the hashtags #brooklyn, #peopleofnewyork, and #ilovenewyork. The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #SocialDistancing and #mask which were common during that time. The image shows a fountain or water feature in what appears to be a Brooklyn park, suggesting the person is not a tourist but rather someone documenting their local area. The hashtag #newyorkstrong also suggests a sense of community and residence in New York. These factors together indicate the person is likely a resident of Brooklyn/New York rather than a visitor, and would therefore stay more than 30 days.", "id": "1303044920408707073"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted on September 7, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags like #stayhome and #quarantinelife, suggesting the person is residing at this location rather than visiting temporarily. The phrase \"Still going...\" along with the image of home-grown vegetables (eggplants and green beans) in a basket indicates an ongoing gardening activity that has been happening for some time and will continue. Growing vegetables requires weeks to months of cultivation. Additionally, the tweet is geotagged at Charlotte, North Carolina, suggesting this is the author's home location where they are gardening during quarantine, not a temporary trip destination. These factors strongly suggest the author is a resident who will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1303082954101579781"}, {"response": "Option: 1. less than 24 hours.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions \"end the holiday weekend\" and specifically refers to \"#labordayweekend\" and \"#3dayweekend\" while showing people playing golf. The text indicates the author is finishing up their Labor Day weekend activities by playing 18 holes of golf. The tweet was posted on September 7, 2020, which was Labor Day (the last day of the holiday weekend). Since they're describing this as the end of their weekend stay and specifically mentioning it as a way to conclude their holiday, they're likely returning home after this golf outing, suggesting they'll be leaving Clifton within 24 hours of posting the tweet.", "id": "1303143368499789824"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Fountain Valley, California in September 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags #getittogether, #itstheendoftheworldasweknowit, #COVID, and #coronavirus suggest the person is experiencing the ongoing pandemic situation. The image text expressing frustration about \"getting my shit together and the world ends\" indicates the person is likely a resident of Fountain Valley dealing with the extended reality of pandemic life, not just visiting briefly. Given that this was posted during widespread lockdowns and restrictions, and there's no indication of travel or temporary stay, the person is most likely living in Fountain Valley and would remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1303243730833608704"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who lives in Phoenix rather than someone who is visiting. The author is commenting on the seasonal transition from summer to fall in Phoenix, discussing the weather patterns they've experienced (hottest July and August on record) along with COVID-19 problems in the area. The tweet is dated September 8, 2020, and the casual way they reference local weather conditions and ongoing issues suggests this is their home rather than a temporary visit. The image shows what appears to be a sunset view by a body of water in Phoenix, which further suggests the author is a resident enjoying local scenery. During the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, most people were staying in their primary residences, and there's no indication this is a temporary stay.", "id": "1303329076816556032"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet shows the person attending a live music event at Terraza 7 in Queens, NY. Given that the tweet is dated September 8, 2020 (during the COVID-19 pandemic), and the location is Queens, NY, it appears this is likely a local venue for the Twitter user. The hashtags #queens and #queensrocks suggest a connection to the area. There's no indication of travel or visiting - instead, this appears to be someone enjoying local entertainment in their home neighborhood. The fact that they're attending local entertainment during the pandemic suggests they are a resident of Queens rather than a visitor. Therefore, they will likely stay in Queens for more than 30 days as it appears to be their place of residence.", "id": "1303405395705311232"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the author is taking street photography in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, and based on the hashtags (#covid_19, #covid19, #coronavirus, #corona), this was during the COVID-19 pandemic (confirmed by the September 8, 2020 date). The tweet suggests the author is a local photographer capturing scenes from their car in their own neighborhood rather than a visitor. The fact that they're specifically identifying it as \"Orthodox Williamsburg NYC\" and providing location context suggests familiarity with the area. Since they appear to be a Brooklyn resident documenting their community during the pandemic, they would likely stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1303418624963751938"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Los Angeles in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The person is showing off their new mask from MOCA (Museum of Contemporary Art, Los Angeles) and using hashtags like #supportthearts and #wearadamnmask. The location tag at Clark Street Echo Park suggests they are in a familiar neighborhood in Los Angeles. There's no indication of travel or visiting - rather, it appears this person is a local resident supporting a local arts institution by purchasing their mask. Given the context and that they're in their home city during the pandemic, they would likely stay in Los Angeles for more than 30 days after posting this tweet.", "id": "1303441242907385857"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet doesn't indicate that the person is traveling or visiting Gaylord temporarily. Instead, it suggests normal daily life activities during the COVID-19 pandemic. The person mentions making dinner with ingredients from a shopping trip to H Mart \"a couple weeks ago\" and references \"delayed plans (thanks covid),\" which suggests they are living in Gaylord rather than just visiting. The casual nature of the dinner preparation and reference to COVID-related disruptions align with someone who resides in the location rather than someone on a short trip. Given the COVID-19 pandemic context mentioned in the tweet (dated September 2020), it's likely the person is living in Gaylord for an extended period of more than 30 days.", "id": "1303479043447283712"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet appears to be from a Queens, NY resident (posted \"from Queens, NY\") taking a night photo of the NYC skyline from what seems to be the LIC Broadwalk (as indicated by the hashtag). The caption refers to \"This quiet moment right before the 2nd wave hits\" with hashtags including #covid and #newyorklife, suggesting the person lives in New York and is commenting on the upcoming second wave of COVID-19. The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the pandemic when many residents were staying in their home cities long-term. The various New York-related hashtags (#nyc, #newyorklife) further suggest this is the author's home rather than a temporary visit. Therefore, they would likely stay in the city for more than 30 days as a resident.", "id": "1303483861079216129"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and contains hashtags like #distancelearning, #teachersofinstagram, and #zoomclasses, which indicate the person is working from home as a teacher. The caption mentions \"working from home\" and shows someone who appears to be in their residence in New York. The professional nature of the work (teaching) and the established work-from-home setup suggest this is not a temporary stay but rather their permanent residence where they're conducting online classes during the pandemic. Given the context of the pandemic in 2020 when remote teaching was implemented for extended periods, the person is likely to stay in New York for more than 30 days.", "id": "1303526000626728960"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet includes hashtags like #stayhome and #staysafe, which were commonly used during the COVID-19 pandemic to encourage people to remain at home. The tweet was posted on September 9, 2020, which was during the height of the pandemic when stay-at-home orders were common. Additionally, the location tag shows Oak Park (in San Diego, CA), suggesting this is likely the person's residential area rather than a vacation destination. The hashtags #mylife and #myheart also imply this is where they live regularly, not a temporary visit. Given these factors and the context of the pandemic, it's likely the person is residing in San Diego for an extended period exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1303730755420344320"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Bradenton, FL on September 9, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. There is no indication in the tweet that the person is traveling or visiting Bradenton temporarily. The tweet is about an aircraft that has been repurposed for transporting medical PPE and masks, not about the author's own travel plans. Since the tweet appears to be shared from the author's home location (Bradenton) during the pandemic, and there's no mention of travel plans, it's reasonable to assume the author is a resident of Bradenton and will stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1303738283768254465"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet refers to \"Covid school days at the park\" which suggests the author lives in Greenville with their children who are attending school there during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags like #mammasworld and #middlechildmagic suggest this is about family life, and #yeahthatgreenville and #greenville360 indicate a connection to the local community. This appears to be showing a regular park visit during the pandemic as part of their normal life in Greenville, not a temporary trip. The context of \"Covid school days\" also suggests this is an ongoing situation where they're finding ways to make the pandemic restrictions more bearable, indicating they are residents who will stay there well beyond 30 days.", "id": "1303857281063362561"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet refers to \"The new normal\" and includes hashtags related to toys, unboxing, and collecting, along with imagery representing COVID-19 (green virus particles, face masks, and PPE references). This suggests the author is likely a resident of Chicago, not a visitor. The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, and the content reflects adapting to pandemic life rather than temporary travel. The toy collecting theme and reference to a \"new normal\" indicates this is the author's ongoing lifestyle in their home city, not a temporary visit. Therefore, they would be staying in Chicago for more than 30 days.", "id": "1303865284856557568"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes the hashtag #quarantine. The user appears to be at home in Brooklyn (specifically Williamsburg), playing with their cat/kitten. The hashtags #nyc and #brooklyn suggest this is their place of residence rather than a temporary location. The image shows a domestic scene with a kitten playing in what appears to be a home setting with a cat tunnel toy. Given that the tweet indicates this is during quarantine period in their home city, and considering the pandemic circumstances in September 2020, it's likely the person will stay there for more than 30 days as this appears to be their permanent residence.", "id": "1304173885160648707"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from someone who lives and works in San Ramon, California. This is indicated by the comparison between how they look for streaming versus how they look for work (with protective gear). The hashtags #staysafe and #wearamask along with the face shield in the \"work\" image suggest this is their regular workplace during the COVID-19 pandemic. The tweet is dated September 10, 2020, during the pandemic when many people were working from their permanent residences. There's no indication of travel or temporary stay - instead, this appears to be showing their regular life in San Ramon, where they both work and stream content. Therefore, they will likely remain in San Ramon for more than 30 days.", "id": "1304204498823512067"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (September 11, 2020) from Richland, WA. The person is reminiscing about visiting a bakery five years ago, and mentions missing \"going out for sure\" with hashtags like #covid19 and #pandemicssuck. This strongly suggests the person is a resident of Richland or the Tri-Cities area (as indicated by the #tricitieswa hashtag) who is currently unable to go out due to pandemic restrictions. They're not visiting Richland - they appear to live there and are experiencing the extended lockdown periods common during the pandemic. The nostalgic tone about a past activity in the same city suggests they're a long-term resident who will stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1304316417819410432"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Los Angeles on September 11, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The caption \"Sigh. I guess we're still doing this\" alongside the hashtag #covid19 and the image of a statue wearing a mask suggests the author is referring to ongoing mask-wearing requirements and pandemic restrictions. The tone indicates resignation to a continuing situation rather than a temporary visit. The location tag of Sherman Oaks (a neighborhood in Los Angeles) and the fact that the tweet shows the author is already in Los Angeles suggests they are a resident rather than a visitor. During this period of the pandemic, stay-at-home orders and restrictions were common for extended periods, indicating the author is likely to remain in Los Angeles for more than 30 days.", "id": "1304582331618004992"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet appears to be from someone who is likely a resident of Baldwinsville, New York, not a visitor. Several factors suggest this:\n\n1. The image shows a person in what appears to be their home (indoor setting with personal decor visible)\n2. There's no indication of travel or visiting in the tweet text\n3. The hashtags reference COVID-19 during September 2020, when many people were staying in their home locations\n4. The person is showing off a themed face mask (Spaceballs reference), which suggests everyday pandemic life rather than travel\n5. The location tag seems to be indicating their current residence rather than a temporary destination\n\nSince there's no indication of a trip or temporary stay, and the context suggests this is where the person lives during the pandemic, they would likely remain in Baldwinsville for more than 30 days after posting.", "id": "1304597953861033984"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThis tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (September 12, 2020) from Los Angeles, and the author uses hashtags like #stayhome and #stayinside, suggesting they are residing there rather than visiting. The tweet references wildfire season and smokey skies in California, showing they're documenting environmental conditions where they live. The location tag \"Valley Glen, Los Angeles\" indicates this is their home area. There's no indication of travel plans or that they're temporarily visiting. During this period of the pandemic, residents were generally staying in their permanent locations, especially with the additional warning about wildfire conditions making it advisable to remain at home.", "id": "1304798359346016256"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet doesn't indicate that the person is traveling to or visiting Costa Mesa. Rather, the tweet is posted from Costa Mesa (as shown in the location timestamp \"from Costa Mesa, CA\"), suggesting this is where the person is based. The content of the tweet indicates the person is reminiscing about flying while cleaning out their phone during the COVID-19 pandemic (with hashtags #COVID, #COVID19, #CORONAVIRUS). The person is sharing an aerial nighttime city view photo, likely from a past flight, while expressing that they miss flying. Since this was posted in September 2020, during significant COVID restrictions, and there's no indication of travel plans, it appears the person is settled in Costa Mesa for an extended period, likely exceeding 30 days.", "id": "1304890764518928385"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during the COVID-19 pandemic (September 2020) from Kailua, Hawaii, with hashtags like #luckywelivehawaii and #lockdown. The phrase \"No other place I'd rather be!\" and the hashtag #luckywelivehawaii strongly suggest the person lives in Hawaii rather than just visiting. This indicates they are a resident of Kailua/Hawaii, not a tourist. The beautiful beach image shows where they're spending time during lockdown, but as a local resident, they would be staying there for more than 30 days. The combination of COVID-era context, residency implications in the hashtags, and posting from the location itself all point to long-term residence.", "id": "1304904673862787072"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during September 2020, which was during the COVID-19 pandemic. The author is in Dallas, TX and based on the hashtags (#covid19) and the mention of being \"in a Covid world,\" this appears to be where they live rather than a temporary visit. The tweet seems to be about getting dressed up for a Saturday night out at a local venue (Clint and Bill's Adult Water Park) rather than being on a trip. The hashtags like #transisbeautiful and #transvisibility suggest personal identity expression rather than travel purposes. Additionally, during the COVID-19 pandemic, extended stays at home locations were common. There is no indication this is a short trip or temporary stay, so it's likely the person resides in Dallas and will remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1304943599432404993"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet suggests that the person is at Canarsie, which is a neighborhood in Brooklyn, NY. Based on the tweet content, several factors indicate this is likely their home or regular location rather than a temporary visit:\n\n1. The tweet mentions \"Hanging with my #Queen\" and references a \"Baby dedication\" ceremony, suggesting family-based activities in their community\n2. The hashtag #ministrylife indicates this is part of their regular pastoral work\n3. The location tag shows Canarsie (in Brooklyn), and the tweet itself shows it was posted from Brooklyn, NY\n4. The date (September 13, 2020) was during the COVID-19 pandemic, when travel was limited and most people were staying in their primary residences\n5. The context suggests this is part of their ongoing ministry work during the pandemic rather than a short visit\n\nThese factors together strongly suggest the person lives in Brooklyn and will be staying there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1305211498780921857"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be promoting clothing merchandise (a purple \"2020 Pandemic\" hoodie and swimming trunks) from what seems to be the person's own clothing brand or a brand they're affiliated with. The tweet is posted from Los Angeles, CA on September 13, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags like #brand, #clothingline, and #clothingbrand suggest this is related to a business based in Los Angeles. Since this appears to be a business/promotional post from their home location rather than a travel post, and considering it was during the pandemic when people generally stayed in their home locations, it's most likely the person lives in Los Angeles and would remain there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1305287737784770560"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates that the person is in Portland (PDX) at Montavilla, and mentions this is the \"first time since #covid19\" they've had to put a sweatshirt on, suggesting they live in this area rather than just visiting. The Bureau of Police Portland sweatshirt visible in the image further suggests the person has local connections. They're not describing a trip or vacation, but rather commenting on a weather change in what appears to be their home city. Given the context of the COVID-19 pandemic in September 2020 and the casual observation about local weather, this indicates the person is a resident who will stay in Portland for more than 30 days.", "id": "1305301313660489729"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Honolulu, Hawaii with hashtags including #marriedlife, #husband, and #lockdown, which suggests the person lives there rather than just visiting. The mention of lockdown (posted in September 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic) indicates they are residing in Waikiki, Honolulu during this period. The homemade dinner (steaks and pasta with different sauces) prepared together with their spouse Taylor also suggests a home setting rather than a temporary visit. The combination of these factors strongly indicates they are residents who will stay more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1305383788277424128"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Washington, DC and mentions \"The White House\" as a location tag, suggesting the author is likely a resident of the area rather than a visitor. There is no indication in the tweet that the author is traveling or visiting temporarily. Given that the tweet was posted during September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and includes hashtags related to politics and the 2020 election, it appears the author is commenting on current events from their home location. The political nature of the content and mention of The White House suggests the author is familiar with and likely lives in Washington, DC. Therefore, they would likely stay in the city for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1305395456701329408"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet is from September 15, 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and appears to be posted during the author's regular morning commute in Queens, NY (as indicated by hashtags #morningcommute and location tag for the 7 Train). The author seems to be a local resident commenting on another commuter who is bringing a bike onto the subway without wearing a mask. The hashtags (#nyct, #commuterproblems) and the casual observation of other commuters suggest this is part of the author's regular routine in their home city rather than a temporary visit. Therefore, the author is likely a resident who will stay in the city for more than 30 days.", "id": "1305885290217631746"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet is about setting up a chalkboard for homeschooling with Waldorf education methods, as indicated by hashtags like #homeschool, #waldorfchalkboard, and #waldorfinspired. The image shows a newly installed chalkboard with a step ladder nearby, suggesting this is a permanent installation for ongoing homeschooling activities. Since this was posted in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic when many families were establishing long-term homeschooling setups, and given the permanent nature of installing a chalkboard, the author is likely planning to stay in Bennington for an extended period (more than 30 days) to conduct homeschooling.", "id": "1306077284886032384"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted from Manhattan, NY in September 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic. The hashtags like #pod, #familypod, and #chosenfamily suggest the person is with their social bubble or \"pod\" - a common practice during the pandemic where people limited social interactions to a small trusted group. The image shows people enjoying time on a boat in New York, and the person refers to this as their \"chosen family.\" The hashtags #covid_19 and #safetyfirst indicate pandemic awareness. Since the tweet is posted from Manhattan and there's no indication of travel or visiting (like vacation hashtags), it appears the person is a resident of New York City who is spending time with their social pod. During this period of the pandemic, most people were staying in their primary residences for extended periods. Therefore, it's most likely the author will remain in Manhattan for more than 30 days.", "id": "1306255513596956681"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted during September 2020, which was during the COVID-19 pandemic when extended lockdowns were common. The person mentions \"this quarantine has gained some weight,\" suggesting they have already been in quarantine for a considerable time and will likely continue to stay in Washington D.C. for an extended period. Since quarantine periods during COVID-19 often lasted more than 30 days, especially in major cities like Washington D.C., and there's no indication that the person is traveling or planning to leave soon, it's reasonable to conclude they will remain there for more than 30 days after posting the tweet.", "id": "1306319405958270977"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted in September 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The user is expressing pandemic fatigue and uses hashtags related to ongoing pandemic lifestyle changes (#distancing, #facecovering, #virtualschool, #virtualmeetings). The barbed wire hula hoop image is a metaphor for the harsh, uncomfortable reality of 2020. Given that the tweet is from Genesee, MI and references pandemic conditions that were ongoing with no immediate end in sight, it's likely the person lives in Genesee rather than visiting temporarily. The pandemic restrictions and virtual activities mentioned suggest they were experiencing an extended stay that would continue for more than 30 days as pandemic conditions persisted.", "id": "1306410724202180610"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nThe tweet shows the author's home office setup in Pickerington, Ohio, with hashtags like #homeoffice, #remotework, and #workfromhome. The date of the tweet (September 17, 2020) is during the COVID-19 pandemic when many people were working remotely for extended periods. The author appears to be showing their permanent or semi-permanent work-from-home setup, mentioning they \"always wanted an office with a window,\" suggesting this is a long-term arrangement. The suburban setting and view through window blinds reinforces the impression that this is their residence where they'll be working for an extended period, likely more than 30 days.", "id": "1306639679190528000"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a restaurant or eatery called \"The New Oxford Canteen\" in Oxford, MS, showing a sandwich they serve (Curried Chicken & Green Apple Salad on toasted Sourdough). The tweet includes hashtags like \"#wearamask\" and \"#weareallinthistogether\" which were common during the COVID-19 pandemic. The account \"@OxfordCanteen1\" seems to be the restaurant's official account posting about their menu item. This suggests the author is not visiting Oxford temporarily but is based there as a local business. Since this is a local establishment posting about their food offering rather than a traveler, they would likely be staying in Oxford for more than 30 days.", "id": "1306778532413087744"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet appears to be from a local resident of Lynchburg, VA, not someone who is visiting temporarily. The author mentions taking their dog to a local restaurant (The Water Dog) and is focused on socializing their rescue dog, which is a longer-term activity. The hashtag #lynchburgva and the casual mention of local establishments suggests the author lives in Lynchburg. Additionally, the tweet was posted in September 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, and the author references the challenges of going to restaurants during this time, indicating they are dealing with ongoing local restrictions as a resident. There's no indication of travel or visiting plans that would suggest a shorter stay.", "id": "1307055116957233153"}, {"response": "Option: 2. 1-30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet mentions visiting a restaurant called \"The Water Dog\" in Lynchburg, VA, and discusses socializing a dog (likely a Great Pyrenees/Siberian Husky mix based on the hashtags and image). The author is talking about going to restaurants during COVID and socializing their dog, which suggests they are in the area for more than just a day trip but not necessarily for an extended period like permanent residence or long-term quarantine. The activities described (restaurant visits, dog socialization) indicate a stay of several days to a few weeks, fitting within the 1-30 day timeframe.", "id": "1307055312189489154"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet indicates the person is \"trying to adjust to the #newnormal #pandemicfashion\" in Hilliard, Ohio. The hashtags #newnormal and #pandemicfashion, along with wearing protective gear (face shield and mask) suggest this is about adapting to ongoing pandemic conditions in what appears to be their local area. This tweet was posted in September 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic when people were adjusting to long-term lifestyle changes. The person is likely a resident of Hilliard rather than a visitor, as they're adapting to pandemic life there, not just passing through. Given the context of the pandemic and the indication of adjusting to new circumstances in their location, they would likely remain in Hilliard for more than 30 days.", "id": "1307061818213400582"}, {"response": "Option: 3. more than 30 days.\n\nReason: The tweet was posted on September 19, 2020, during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic. The author uses hashtags like #pandemic and refers to this as \"just another\" selfie at home, suggesting they have been staying at home for an extended period. The location is tagged as New York, New York, and the caption indicates this is their home rather than a temporary visit. Since this appears to be where they live rather than a trip (they specifically say \"at home\"), and given the pandemic circumstances when many people were under extended lockdowns, it's most likely they will continue to stay there for more than 30 days.", "id": "1307283711487086592"}]